it ? 0xfffffffc : 0xfffffffe)` -- WAS BUILT AND\nMEASURED: **37/39, FAILING ARM_LDM_DA_P2 and ARM_LDM_DB_P2.**  `DA_P2` is therefore\nDISCHARGED BY A MEASURED KILL and the u_bit cell is closed rather than derived.\n*Six mutants built, six dead.*  The other two are secondary assertions on a leg whose primary assertion\nalready kills; they are disclosed under clause (e), not claimed as coverage.\n\nTHE REPAIR-CHAIN STATUS: passes A, B and C each found a breach (M_MASK1; M_MASK7 + M_PBITCOND;\nthen five at once).  **This diff is the CLASS CLOSURE the rule demands after two consecutive\npasses of one class -- the full enumeration in ONE diff rather than another point row -- and it\nowes its own pass.  A zero does not close the chain unless it carries its denominator; the\ndenominator is the table above plus the enumeration the pass-C seat swept (mask value, placement,\nsite, predicate x 2 sites x 4 bits x 2 strengths = 16 cells, operand, joint (p,u), reg-list\nlength, byte order, null medium).**\n\n---\n\n*** PASS C DID NOT CLOSE THE CHAIN. 2026-09-11 17:52. FOUR SEATS OF SIX FOUND A CLAUSE-(d)\nESCAPE; TWO DID NOT.  `#524` DOES NOT COMMIT. ***\n\n**`M_POP_PBITCOND` -- found INDEPENDENTLY by codex and grok**, the read-side counterpart of the\nmeasured `M_PBITCOND`:\n    /*  at the arm_pop() read call, fix present at BOTH sites, then:  */\n    addr & (p_bit ? 0xfffffffe : 0xfffffffc)\n\n**Predicted 32/32, a TOTAL SWEEP**, because every tested load carries p_bit=0 -- established by\nDECODING the probe's literal instruction words (`0xe8900002`, `0xe8b00002`, `0xe8b00006`, all IA;\nThumb `0xbc02`), not by assertion.\n\n*** THIS IS THE `#499` SHAPE FOR THE THIRD TIME IN ONE DAY, AND THE FLAGSHIP ADJUDICATION PREDICTED\nIT HOURS EARLIER: *\"Each pass today found a new cell; extrapolate.\"* ***  Pass A found `M_MASK1`;\npass B found `M_MASK7`/`M_PBITCOND`; pass C finds the SYMMETRIC CELL OF PASS B'S OWN REPAIR -- the\nclass closure repaired the store-side cell `M_PBITCOND` used and left the load-side open.\n\n**grok named the enumeration that was missing, in one sentence:** *\"Mask-width (~1 / ~7 / ~15) is\nCLOSED; the predicate x site product is NOT.\"*  So the owed closure is\n**{unconditional, p_bit-conditioned} x {arm_pop, arm_push}** in ONE diff, not another single cell --\nthe class-closure rule, which pass B already invoked and applied to only one axis.\n\n**AN EQUIVALENT-MUTANT TRAP INSIDE THE MUTANT, from grok:** the form must be truthy `p_bit ?`, NOT\n`p_bit == 1`.  `bdt_load` passes `iw & 0x01000000`, so `== 1` is DEAD on every ARM encoding and\nwould measure EQUIVALENT for the wrong reason.\n\n*** IT IS A PREDICTION AND IS RECORDED AS OWED, NOT AS A SCORE. ***  Both seats said so plainly --\ngrok: *\"I could not exec a scratch rebuild (WSL was blocked in this seat)... treat the 32/32 as a\npredicted sweep that still owes a clone run.\"*  Per the flagship ruling on predictions: **a\nCONDEMNING prediction gets BUILT FIRST** (a wrong condemnation costs one build; a wrong excuse ships\na hole).  Build `M_POP_PBITCOND`, score it, and if it sweeps that is **L2 -- repair in-round**.\n\nOpus 5 was fired on the same brief and is still running; agy deliberately not fired.\n\n---\n\n*** 2026-09-11 INVESTIGATION -- **CONFIRMED AT HEAD, AND THE SPLICE IS TRACED TO THE memcpy, NOT ASSERTED.  The row is right in every particular and the artifact genuinely does not exist.** ***\n\n**THE MECHANISM, read at HEAD (`cpu_arm_instr.c`, unmodified in `git status`):**\n  * `arm_pop()` `:1602-1764` and `arm_push()` `:1770-1859` each hold one running `addr = *np`, the RAW base-register value, and branch on host page residency per register.\n  * **FAST path** (page resident): `p32[(addr & 0xfff) >> 2]` -- the `>>2` truncates the low two bits, so it is **architecturally correct** for the implemented A=0/U=0 state (DDI0100I LDM(1) p.A4-37 / STM(1) p.A4-190: *ignore the least significant two bits of the address*).\n  * **SLOW path** (cold): `cpu->memory_rw(..., addr, data, 4, ...)` with the **RAW, UNMASKED addr** (`:1653-1654`, `:1842-1843`).  **Traced into `memory_rw.c:728-782`: `offset = paddr & offset_mask` is PAGE-granularity, NOT word-rounded, and the transfer is a raw `memcpy` at `:770-772`/`:780-782`.  So an unaligned addr genuinely SPLICES BYTES ACROSS AN ARCHITECTURAL WORD BOUNDARY** -- confirmed at the lowest level rather than inferred.\n\n> **The same instruction, the same unaligned base, produces two different byte patterns purely according to HOST PAGE-CACHE WARMTH -- a condition invisible to the guest architecture.**\n\n*** AND THE WRITEBACK IS CORRECT AS IT STANDS, WHICH IS WHY THE FIX SHAPE MATTERS: *** `if (w_bit) *np = addr;` uses the unmasked running addr, and **Mode 4 writeback DERIVES from unmasked Rn**.  **So the repair must mask ONLY at the two `memory_rw` call sites and NEVER at entry** -- masking at entry corrupts writeback.\n\n**THE IN-TREE PRECEDENT FOR EXACTLY THAT SHAPE ALREADY EXISTS:** `src/cpus/generate_arm_multi.c:117` emits `addr &= 0xffc;` -- the code-generated fast dispatch already masks the ACCESS while leaving writeback unmasked.  *A transferred warrant, verified at the cited line.*\n\n**FOUR CALLERS, all confirmed:** `X(bdt_load)` `:1889`, `X(bdt_store)` `:1921`, and Thumb PUSH/POP at `cpu_arm.c:1670`/`:1677`.  **Thumb inherits the defect and has ZERO probe coverage of any kind.**\n\n=== NO ARTIFACT EXISTS.  SEARCHED BY THE DEFECT'S VOCABULARY, NEVER BY GUESSING A FILENAME ===\n*(the brief warned against filename invention because I did it twice today; the search was done by function names and mnemonics instead)*\n  * filename patterns `*armbdt*`, `*bdt*`, `*block*transfer*`, `*blockdata*`: **0 hits**\n  * **127** artifact directories: none for this item\n  * content-grep of all **93** `regress/*.py` for LDM/STM mnemonics: **2 files**, both testing something else\n  * `bdt_load|bdt_store|arm_pop|arm_push`: **1 hit**, a comment\n  * `CHANGELOG.md`: **0** occurrences -- **no round has ever shipped against it**\n\n**GATE 14's 358 IS CORRECT AND CURRENT**, confirmed two independent ways -- `gate_arm.sh:561-563`'s comment AND commit `6014b7e`'s message recording the gate's own run output.  *(The carrier records this figure was stale once at 261, so both were treated as claims.)*  **And it does not cover this defect under any name:**\n  * `arm_writeback_probe.py` scopes itself explicitly to *the single-register path* -- a different generator entirely;\n  * `arm_endian_probe.py` DOES reach `arm_pop`/`arm_push`, but only on byte order and **with a word-aligned base (`0x10000`), where masking is a no-op** -- so it cannot discriminate this defect whichever path a row takes;\n  * measured corroboration: three binaries (HEAD / mask-at-access / mask-at-entry) all scored **22/22 identical** on `arm_writeback_probe.py`, **including the entry-mask mutant that corrupts writeback**;\n  * and the regress adjudication of `6014b7e` states it plainly -- *gate_arm passed 16/16 WITH armbdt PRESENT.*\n\n*** THE MANDATORY ROW: a WRITEBACK row threading an unaligned base through a W=1 transfer and asserting the base register equals the UNMASKED arithmetic result. ***  It is the row `arm_writeback_probe.py` is structurally blind to, and **the only one that discriminates the correct fix from the plausible-but-wrong mask-at-entry mutant.**\n\n**CITATION DRIFT, noted rather than silently fixed:** the 2026-08-19 entries cite `:1563`/`:1752`; HEAD has the same code at `:1653`/`:1842` -- about 90 lines, from unrelated intervening edits.  The mechanism is unchanged; only the numbers moved.\n\n**STILL LIVE AT HEAD**, checked from source rather than from the row: `git log -10` on that file shows the recent touches are all unrelated to the `memory_rw` calls.\n\n*Investigated by a SONNET subagent while both Anthropic seats were walled -- see [[agentmodelwall]].  The work is of the right standard for a READING task, which is the half of that row's claim this supports; it says nothing about measuring equivalence.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 WITNESS + KILL TABLE (agy 3.8, measuring seat, independent transport).  **THE WITNESS EXISTS.  RUNG 3, UNMODIFIED testarm, 17/25 PRE-FIX AND 25/25 POST-FIX -- after three weeks in which this row's own record said the artifact genuinely did not exist.** ***\n\n**THE DEFECT IS UNCHANGED AND IS NOW MEASURED RATHER THAN DERIVED.**  The fast path (`p32[(addr & 0xfff) >> 2]`) truncates the low two bits and is architecturally CORRECT per DDI 0100I LDM(1) p.A4-37 / STM(1) p.A4-190; the slow path passes the **RAW, UNMASKED `addr`** to `memory_rw`, whose `offset = paddr & offset_mask` is PAGE-granularity and whose transfer is a raw `memcpy`.  **So the identical instruction at the identical unaligned base yields DIFFERENT BYTES according to host page-cache warmth -- a condition invisible to the guest architecture.**\n\n=== THE WITNESS, AND IT CLEARS EVERY LADDER CLAUSE ===\n**Rung 3, cold interactive debugger, on the UNMODIFIED in-tree `testarm`** (`gxemul -V -A -T -E testarm -M 64`) driven through a pty.  **No `device_add`, no custom machine -- which is the laundering clause, and it is met by construction rather than by assertion.**\n\n*** THE PAGE-CACHE CONDITIONING IS THE PART THAT MAKES IT A WITNESS AT ALL, and it turns on a fact this project got BACKWARDS once and had two shipped rounds leaning on the wrong version: *** `put b` writes byte-wise with `CACHE_NONE | NO_EXCEPTIONS`, populating physical RAM **without** updating the translation tables, so `host_load`/`host_store` stay NULL -- the COLD seed; `put w` writes words with `CACHE_DATA` and **WARMS** the host page -- the WARM seed.  *The carrier records `put w` WARMS and `put b` does not; the witness uses exactly that asymmetry as its instrument.*\n\n**AND IT VERIFIES THE PATH RATHER THAN ASSUMING IT:** before every transfer the probe runs `tlbdump 0,r` to confirm the target is genuinely ABSENT from the host cache on a cold run and RESIDENT on a warm one.  **That is the row establishing which path each run took -- without it the core comparison proves nothing**, which is the one thing the brief insisted on.\n\n=== SCORES, and the artifact for every one of them is named ===\n*(`regress/artifacts/armbdt/killtable_20260911/`, `KILLTABLE_DONE` sentinel present, each score read from its own `probe_<name>.raw`)*\n\n| leg | score | reading |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BASE` (fix applied) | **25/25** | clean |\n| `HEAD` (committed, pre-fix) | **17/25** | all four cold-data rows AND all four divergence checks red -- *test-first satisfied: the symptom is shown on the COMMITTED build and goes green post-fix with the same script* |\n| `M_ENTRY` (mask at entry) | **20/25** | *** KILLED, AND KILLED BY THE WRITEBACK ROWS SPECIFICALLY *** |\n| `M_POP_ONLY` (one site masked) | 21/25 | killed |\n| `M_PUSH_ONLY` (other site masked) | 21/25 | killed |\n| `M_NULL_MEDIUM` (identity) | **6/25** | 19 rows red -- the rows genuinely observe block transfer, discharging clause (c) |\n| `RESTORED` | **17/25** | *** IDENTICAL TO `HEAD`, which is the clean-restore proof: the clone came back to the committed baseline exactly. *** |\n\n*** `M_ENTRY` IS THE VINDICATION OF THE MANDATORY ROW. ***  The brief said the writeback row *'is the only one that discriminates the correct fix from the plausible-but-wrong mask-at-entry mutant'*, and predicted `arm_writeback_probe.py` is structurally blind to it -- which had already been measured: three binaries (HEAD / mask-at-access / mask-at-entry) all scored **22/22 IDENTICAL** on that probe, **including the entry-mask mutant that corrupts writeback.**  The new instrument kills it 20/25.  *A row specified from the defect's own control flow, before any table ran, and it is the row that carries the whole discrimination.*\n\n**ORDER PROVEN BY MTIME, not asserted:** `PRETABLE.out` written **06:46:22**, first table run `probe_BASE.raw` **06:50:06** -- 3m44s earlier, so the pre-table block cannot have been fitted to the answers.\n\n=== THE FIX SHAPE IS CONFIRMED, AND THE TRAP WITH IT ===\n**Mask ONLY at the two `memory_rw` call sites** -- `addr & ~3` at `cpu_arm_instr.c:1653` and `:1842`.  **NEVER at entry:** `if (w_bit) *np = addr;` (`:1715`, `:1858`) uses the unmasked running `addr`, and Mode 4 writeback derives from unmasked `Rn`, so an entry mask corrupts writeback -- which is precisely what `M_ENTRY` measures.  In-tree precedent verified at the cited line: `generate_arm_multi.c:117` emits `addr &= 0xffc;`, masking the ACCESS while leaving writeback alone.\n\n*** HOUSE RULES OBSERVED, VERIFIED BY ME RATHER THAN TAKEN FROM THE REPORT: `git status` shows `src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c` UNMODIFIED in GXEMUL-SEC. ***  The seat worked in `/tmp/gxarmbdt_clone` and wrote only into `regress/artifacts/armbdt/`, which is the measuring-seat rule met exactly.  The live `/tmp/gxop519p2` tail was not touched.\n\n**STATUS: the fix is SPECIFIED AND MEASURED BUT NOT APPLIED TO SEC.**  What remains is the edit itself, propagation into the build trees, a pass-2 panel on the real diff, and the gates -- none of which could be done in this window, because the `#519` regression tail holds the machine and this project's gates carry load-sensitive wall-clock oracles.  *Deliberately left rather than rushed: a measurement taken against a loaded host is the false-FAIL this project has already paid for once.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 IMPLEMENTED.  **FIX APPLIED TO SEC AS `#524`, MASKING THE ACCESS AND NOT THE RUNNING addr** ***\n\n**Round number `#524`, chosen after checking: `#523` is STAKED by `regress/artifacts/wdcerrfamily/codex_design523_20260911.txt` even though it appears in no CHANGELOG entry, source file or commit.**  *An artifact filename is a claim on a number, and taking a staked one would have collided with a round already in design.*\n\n**THE EDIT, both sites, `uint32_t addr` masked with a hex literal to match the file's own idiom (`addr & 0xfff`, `addr &= 0xffc`):**\n    cpu_arm_instr.c  arm_pop()  : cpu->memory_rw(..., addr & 0xfffffffc, data, ..., MEM_READ, ...)\n    cpu_arm_instr.c  arm_push() : cpu->memory_rw(..., addr & 0xfffffffc, data, ..., MEM_WRITE, ...)\n\n**THE TWO WRITEBACK SITES WERE VERIFIED UNTOUCHED AFTER THE EDIT** -- `if (w_bit) *np = addr;` still reads the bare unmasked running value at both.  *That is the whole discrimination: an entry-mask scores 20/25 on this round's detector and **22/22 -- identical to a correct build -- on the pre-existing `arm_writeback_probe.py`.*  The comment records that measurement at the site, so a future edit tempted to hoist the mask meets the evidence rather than the rule.\n\n*Other `memory_rw(..., addr, ...)` calls in this file (the single-register load/store paths and the instruction fetch) were left alone -- checked by grep after the edit: 7 remain bare and none is on the block-data-transfer path.*\n\n**PROPAGATION, and it is deliberately PARTIAL:**\n  * `est/` -- **DONE and verified BYTE-IDENTICAL by `cmp`.**  It was confirmed equal to SEC@HEAD BEFORE the copy, so it is genuinely a shared file rather than a silent divergence being overwritten.  *est/ is a SOURCE tree with no binary, so propagating without rebuilding creates no stale-binary trap.*\n  * `build/`, `build-asan/`, `/tmp/gxsec-build` -- **NOT propagated.**  Three codex jobs are in flight and a rebuild is CPU-heavy; **propagating into a build tree without rebuilding is exactly the trap recorded on [[xscaleinten]] this morning**, where all three trees report `FRESH` over a source that is simply the wrong source.  Recorded in `_scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md` so a fresh session finds the debt.\n\n**STILL OWED BEFORE THIS COMMITS: propagate+rebuild the three build trees together, a pass 2 on the real diff, and the targeted gate.**  The witness and the kill table are already done (HEAD 17/25, fix 25/25, `M_ENTRY` 20/25, `M_NULL_MEDIUM` 6/25, `RESTORED` = HEAD).\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PASS 2 on the real `#524` diff (codex GPT-6 Astra at max).  **PASS 2: NOT RATIFIED -- THE MASKS ARE RIGHT AND 'ONLY TWO SITES' WAS WRONG.** ***\n\n*VERDICT: NOT RATIFIED -- the two masks are locally correct, but other BDT implementations still use unrounded addresses, and the architectural justification needs qualification.*\n\n**THE MASK AND THE LITERAL ARE CORRECT.**  `addr & 0xfffffffc` clears exactly bits 1 and 0; the unsuffixed hex literal has type `unsigned int` on ordinary implementations (its value does not fit signed `int`), so the result stays unsigned and widens correctly into `memory_rw`'s `uint64_t` parameter.  *A wider host pointer does not change this.*\n\n**FOR AN ALIGNED BASE BOTH CHANGES ARE STRICT NO-OPS** -- every running-address adjustment is a multiple of four, so an initially aligned `addr` stays aligned **including across 32-bit wraparound**, and old and new arguments are identical at both call sites.\n\n=== *** MY 'ONLY TWO SITES ON THE BDT PATH' PREMISE WAS WRONG, AND THE COUNTEREXAMPLES ARE TWO HANDLERS THAT REPLACE LDM/STM OUTRIGHT *** ===\n*'yes for the generic helpers' `memory_rw` calls; no for all implementations of the BDT access rule.'*  **Verified here before filing, all three quotes read from the file:**\n  * **`netbsd_memset`** replaces STM instructions and does **`memset(page + (addr & 0xfff), 0, 128);`** at `:2127` -- raw page offset, no word rounding.  Its runtime guards check page bounds, zero source values and page availability; **none rejects an unaligned address.**\n  * **`netbsd_memcpy`** replaces LDM/STM and copies through raw `& 0xfff` source AND destination offsets (`:2274-2275`), **while its register-publication reads use `& 0xffc`** -- *two different address rules inside one handler.*  Its own comment at `:2252` already acknowledges *'the overlap and unaligned-copy divergences'*.\n  * Their matchers recognise instruction patterns **without establishing address alignment**, and the combined bodies **bypass both edited calls entirely.**\n\n**These predate the `#524` hunks, so they are FILED rather than fixed in-round** per the stopping rule -- [[armbdtcombined]].  *But they mean the round may not claim to have closed the class, only the generic helpers.*\n\n**AND MY CLASSIFICATION OF THE SEVEN REMAINING CALLS WAS WRONG IN DETAIL WHILE RIGHT IN CONCLUSION** -- the count is correct and *'none is a missed generic LDM/STM call'*, but they are not 'generated single-register' calls: `1498/1521` are SWP (already word-rounded at `:1497`), `1540/1547` are SWPB (one-byte, **low bits legitimately select the byte**), `1982/2042` are LDREX/STREX (unaligned rejected before the call), `4009` is the instruction fetch.\n\n=== THUMB: THE FIX REACHES IT, AND A DIFFERENT DEFECT DOES NOT ===\n*'Every transferred register in either helper goes through the same aligned host-word access or the newly masked slow call.  There is no Thumb-specific raw-address branch inside either transfer loop.'*  **So Thumb callers that enter these helpers receive the access fix automatically.**\n\n*Coverage unverified because `cpu_arm.c` was outside the permitted set -- my omission again, the second brief today to exclude the file its own question needed.*  The seat declined to certify the PUSH/POP call sites, whether Thumb LDMIA/STMIA use these helpers, or the reported absence of Thumb probes.\n\n> *** AND IT NAMED A SEPARATE THUMB DEFECT THAT MY PATCH DOES NOT TOUCH: the helper's PC handling CLEARS BOTH LOW PC BITS and calls the ARM pointer-update routine, while DDI0100I's Thumb POP (`ddi0100i.txt:24256`) PRESERVES THE HALFWORD ADDRESS and specifies interworking. ***  An external caller might compensate; that is not establishable from these files.  **Unchanged by `#524` and not claimed by it.**\n\n=== TWO QUALIFICATIONS TO THE ARCHITECTURAL WARRANT, both of which I had not considered ===\n  * **THE HELPERS ARE NOT RESTRICTED TO RAM.**  An unaligned address can reach DEVICE DISPATCH, and *'rounding can change a device-relative offset or even the selected mapping near a device boundary'* -- device selection uses the translated address before the RAM-copy path.\n  * **`ddi0100i.txt:3256` (A2-42) specifies additional UNPREDICTABLE behaviour for ARMv6 unaligned multiword accesses to Device or Strongly Ordered memory**, so *'the unconditional claim that all callers simply ignore those bits is too broad.'*\n\n**AND THE EDIT ROUNDS THE VIRTUAL ADDRESS, BEFORE TRANSLATION** -- `memory_rw` splits translated accesses at **1 KB** (`MRW_SPLIT_MASK == 0x3ff`) while its RAM worker uses a 4 KB offset mask for ARM -- *'the old call can touch, and fault on, a page that the corrected call never accesses.'*  **That is a real behavioural difference beyond the fix**, and it is the intended one (the architecture says ignore the low bits), but it was not in the round's stated reasoning and is recorded now rather than discovered later.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PANEL CONVENED on the combined `#524`/`#525` packet, and **SEAT LEDGER FOR THE #524/#525 PANEL -- THREE SEATS DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED, BY NAME** ***\n\n**FIRED: the eight scriptable transports** via `bash GXEMUL-SEC/tools/panel.sh <absolute brief>` -- codex GPT-6 Astra (max), grok 4.6 (xhigh), kimi 3, and the Ollama cloud seats glm-5.3, deepseek-v4-pro, minimax-m3 (+ the kimi-k3 substitute if configured).  **All four Ollama transports were confirmed REACHABLE by `api/tags` beforehand**, so a non-answer from any of them is a seat failure rather than a dead daemon.\n\n=== *** DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED -- NAMED, BECAUSE A SEAT HELD BACK AND A SEAT THAT FAILED ARE DIFFERENT THINGS AND CONFLATING THEM IS HOW A TWO-SEAT REVIEW GETS REPORTED AS A PANEL *** ===\n  * **agy 3.8** -- RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by standing owner ruling, and it is the reason both of these rounds have witnesses at all: it built the `armbdt` witness + kill table (25/25) and the `wdcaltdigin` witness + kill table (16/16) earlier today.  *Spending its bounded budget on a reading seven other seats can do is spending the roster's only independent-transport measuring capability on a fungible job.*\n  * **Opus 5** -- HELD.  It answers through the Agent tool and consumes the same Anthropic quota the owner wants preserved for the adjudication.  *Not a failure, not agreement: a budget decision.*\n  * **Fable 5** -- HELD BY EXPLICIT OWNER SEQUENCING: *'don't run fable until all other panel seats have had a say.'*  **This panel is the 'all other seats' step.**  Its availability is to be checked by a plain subagent rather than by Opus when the time comes.\n\n**WHY THE PACKET IS SHAPED AS IT IS:** every fact is INLINED -- both diffs, both full enclosing contexts, both measured kill tables, and **both first-pass NOT-RATIFIED findings** -- because the Ollama transports cannot read files and a brief containing *'check the source'* is a documented way to turn a live seat into a stall that looks exactly like a quota wall.  *The packet also asks the seats to attack the FIRST PASS'S FINDINGS, not only the code, so the corrections get adversarial scrutiny too.*\n\n*** THE LOAD-BEARING QUESTION PUT TO THEM IS THE SELECTOR ONE, and it is imported from a sibling round rather than invented: *** `sgirandomcolour` had **six mutants score a TOTAL ESCAPE** because every detector row probed a single position and each mutant conditioned the repair's effect on a tile or coordinate selector.  **`#524` has 25 rows and `#525` has 16, and neither has had a selector audit.**  The panel is asked to name a conditioning that a plausible edit could produce and the rows could not see.\n\n*Landing remains HELD by owner ruling until the full panel reports -- nothing is committed and no gate has run.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PANEL (`_scratchpad/panel_20260911_103402`).  **SIX of seven scriptable seats answered substantively; the kimi CLI is WALLED and its `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute ANSWERED, so that seat is COVERED, not lost.** ***\n\n**Seat sizes and tails verified individually rather than by size alone:** codex 300,614 b (streams its reasoning; the tail is substantive), grok 11,840 b, `ollama_minimax` 12,053 b, `ollama_glm` 9,695 b, `ollama_kimi` 7,601 b, `ollama_deepseek` 4,219 b.  **`kimi.txt` is 350 b ending `See log: ...kimi-code.log` -- the CLI failure signature, NOT a considered non-answer.**\n\n**CONSENSUS ON THE PRODUCT CODE: NEITHER FIX IS WRONG.**  glm -- *'#524 is architecturally correct and its scope is what was overstated'*; minimax -- *'Both are correct, minimal, and correctly scoped'*; `kimi-k3` -- *'`addr & 0xfffffffc` per-access is provably equivalent'*; deepseek -- *'Neither fix is wrong as a local correction, but #524 is incomplete (combined handlers)'*, **independently reproducing the codex finding about `netbsd_memset`/`netbsd_memcpy`.**\n\n=== *** THE SELECTOR QUESTION GOT REAL ANSWERS, AND THEY ARE FLOOR-BREACH CANDIDATES *** ===\nI asked, importing the shape from [[sgirandomcolour]]: *what conditioning could a plausible edit introduce that the 25 and 16 rows never vary?*\n\n| round | proposed conditioning | seat | predicted |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `#525` | **`if (odata & WDCS_DRDY) odata = 0;`** | grok | **16/16 -- TOTAL ESCAPE** |\n| `#525` | `if (d->unit == 0) odata = 0;` -- a DRIVE-UNIT selector | deepseek | passes if every row uses the primary controller |\n| `#524` | `if (cpu->mem->page_size == 4096) addr &= ~3;` -- a constant property of the test map | deepseek | passes; breaks on any other page size |\n\n**grok also corrected my clause-(d) choice:** deleting `odata = 0` is NOT the discriminating mutant, *because `M_NULL_EFFECT` already scored 14/16* -- the detector sees it.  **The one that escapes keeps the assignment and gates it.**  *That is the difference between a mutant the rows catch and a mutant the rows are blind to, and I had picked the first and called it the floor test.*\n\n**PRESCRIPTION (grok):** *'Product diffs can stand.  The detectors cannot ship as the round's \"we pinned the repair\" claim until `#524` has a `+2` and a `+3` (or an anchored `& 3` mutant) and `#525` has one CORE observation with `WDCS_DRDY` clear.'*  **That is an L3 -- an undischarged obligation, where the row is the sole carrier of something the round claims.**\n\n*** AND A MEASURED-VS-PREDICTED DISCREPANCY IN MY OWN ARTIFACT, caught by grok and verified here: `PRETABLE.out:54` predicts `M_NULL_MEDIUM 4/25`; `SCORES.txt` measures **6/25**. ***  *I cited 6/25 -- the measured one -- so no record is wrong, but the PREDICTION sits in a durable artifact where a future reader can cite it as a measurement.  That is the frozen-number class with a two-row error already in the drawer.*  grok's reading of the remainder: *'PATH/ATTRIB-style controls surviving an early `return`'*.\n\n**Seats DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED, by name: agy** (reserved for measurement; it built both witnesses today), **Opus 5** (held -- same Anthropic quota the adjudication needs), **Fable 5** (held by owner sequencing until every other seat has spoken -- **this panel is that step**).\n\n*** 2026-09-11 FABLE 5 ADJUDICATION (`FABLE_ADJ_20260911`).  **SEAT ALIVE -- token AND a verbatim `CHECKPOINT.md` HEAD quote returned, and it re-ran `git log --oneline -1` itself to confirm the checkpoint is not stale.**  Fired ONLY after all seven other scriptable seats had spoken, per owner sequencing, and via a subagent rather than Opus. ***\n\n=== RULING: **HOLD for ONE MEASUREMENT, then repair IN-ROUND.  Grok MIS-GRADED this: it is not L3, it is CLAUSE (d).** ===\n\n*** IT IS THE CARRIER'S OWN WORKED EXAMPLE -- `#461`'s `m_align1`, a mask narrowed `& 3` -> `& 1`. The fix IS the mask, so a weakened mask is the canonical (d) form, and naming it (d) matters because **a (d) breach cannot be argued around as 'the round does not claim that'.** ***\n\n**CHECKED BY THE ADJUDICATOR, not inferred: every unaligned base in `agy_armbdt_witness_20260911.py` is `+1`** -- `:218` `0x20001`, `:281` `0x21001`, POP `0x22001`, PUSH `0x23005 -> 0x23001`.  **For every row `x+1 & ~1 == x+1 & ~3`, so `0xfffffffc -> 0xfffffffe` at both sites is 25/25 BY ARITHMETIC.**\n\n> **But it must still be BUILT AND SCORED, not asserted.**  Grok said plainly it had not built it, and this project's own rule is that *'it would sweep the table' is a MEASUREMENT, never an inference.*  **Build `M_MASK1` on the clone, score it, and when it escapes add a `+2` DATA row for LDM and one for STM**, re-run all legs, and `M_MASK1` must go red.\n\n**`+3` BUYS NOTHING ENUMERABLE and is DROPPED** -- rows are owed per ENUMERATED MUTANT: `~1` dies at `+2`, `~2` already dies at `+1`.  **Disclose residue 3 in the contract instead of rowing it.**  *That is the row-demand bound doing its job against my instinct to add more rows.*\n\n**Operative licence is L1** (nothing has shipped), not L2.\n\n=== *** THE `PRETABLE` 4/25-vs-6/25 GAP IS EXPLAINED, AND THE EXPLANATION IS A REAL DEFECT *** ===\nThe two extra survivors are **`ARM_STM_COLD_CLOBBER` and `THUMB_PUSH_COLD_CLOBBER`**, found by diffing the row list against the FAILING line.  **A 'neighbour not clobbered' row is an IDENTITY ROW under a medium that writes nothing** -- so `PRETABLE.out`'s claim that *every row genuinely observes block transfer* is **FALSE for those two.**  *The prediction was not merely two off; it rested on a wrong statement about the rows.*\n\n**Write `PRETABLE2.out` for the re-table -- do NOT append to `PRETABLE.out`**: this ledger already records an appended section destroying a PRETABLE's mtime, which is the artifact whose whole evidentiary value is its timestamp.\n\n**TWO L4s IN MY OWN COMMENT, BOTH APPLIED:**\n  1. *'DDI0100I says to ignore the low two bits'* was UNQUALIFIED.  **Verified at `ddi0100i.txt:7954-7959`: that holds for `CP15_reg1_Ubit == 0` AND alignment checking DISABLED; `Ubit == 1` faults on every non-word-aligned access.**  And the fork is internally inconsistent -- `cpu_arm.c:129` SETS `ARM_CONTROL_ALIGN` in the default control register, `:474` only PRINTS it, and **nothing consults it.**  *** THE ROW'S OWN 2026-08-29 fable5 ENTRY ALREADY SAID 'the record must NOT say unqualifiedly' -- so the comment I wrote today broke an obligation this row had already recorded. ***\n  2. *'Measured: ... `arm_writeback_probe.py` scores it 22/22'* -- **`22/22` appears in NO measurement artifact.**  Verified: it occurs only in briefs and seat answers; `probe_M_ENTRY.raw` backs the 20/25 alone.  **Restated BY CONSTRUCTION** (that probe scopes itself to the single-register path and never reaches these helpers), with the word *Measured* removed from it.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 TABLES 2 AND 3.  **THE CLAUSE-(d) BREACH IS MEASURED, CLOSED AND RE-MEASURED: M_MASK1 25/25 -> 27/29.** ***\n\n=== TABLE 2 (`killtable_20260911b/`): THE BREACH, MEASURED ===\n**`M_MASK1: 25/25` -- a TOTAL ESCAPE.**  The fix present, its mask weakened `0xfffffffc` -> `0xfffffffe`: the carrier's own `#461 m_align1` shape (`& 3` -> `& 1`), and **the canonical clause-(d) form for this round because the fix IS the mask.**\n\n*Fable predicted it by arithmetic -- every unaligned base the probe drove was `+1`, and `x+1 & ~1 == x+1 & ~3` -- and ruled that it be BUILT anyway, because this project's rule is that 'it would sweep the table' is a MEASUREMENT, never an inference.  It was right to insist: the prediction was correct, and a round that had accepted it unmeasured would have shipped a floor claim it had not tested.*\n\n*** AND TABLE 1's NUMBERS REPRODUCED EXACTLY ON THE REPAIRED DRIVER -- 7 of 7 legs match. ***  **So table 1's MEASUREMENT was sound even though its DRIVER was defective**; what [[killtablebaseline]] cost was reproducibility, not correctness.  *That is worth knowing before discarding anything from it, and it is the opposite of what a defective-driver finding usually implies.*\n\n=== TABLE 3 (`killtable_20260911c/`): THE CLOSURE, RE-MEASURED ===\n\n| leg | score | rows it fails |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BASE` (the fix) | **29/29** | none |\n| `HEAD` | 19/29 | 10 -- the defect rows, including BOTH new `+2` rows |\n| **`M_MASK1`** | **27/29** | *** exactly 2: `ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA_P2`, `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_P2` *** |\n| `M_ENTRY` | 24/29 | 5 -- **ALL FIVE WRITEBACK ROWS AND NOTHING ELSE** |\n| `M_POP_ONLY` | 24/29 | 5 -- the STM-side rows |\n| `M_PUSH_ONLY` | 24/29 | 5 -- the LDM-side MIRROR of those |\n| `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | 8/29 | 21, including BOTH control rows |\n| `RESTORED` | 19/29 | = `HEAD`, the clean-restore proof (now against GIT) |\n\n> *** EVERY LEG DIES ON A DISTINCT, SEMANTICALLY CORRECT SET, AND THAT IS THE PART WORTH MORE THAN THE SCORES. ***  `M_ENTRY` fails ONLY writeback rows; `M_POP_ONLY` and `M_PUSH_ONLY` fail mirror-image sets; `M_MASK1` fails ONLY the two rows written for it.  *Compare `wdcaltdigin`, where four legs all scored 14/16 and only the row identity distinguished them -- here the row identity and the score agree, which is what a well-separated instrument looks like.*\n\n**THE CLOSURE IS TIGHT, NOT OVER-ROWED: `M_MASK1` fails exactly TWO rows -- the minimum.**  *Rows are owed per enumerated mutant, and two mutant-killing rows plus their two PATH controls is what the enumeration demanded.*\n\n=== THE PREDICTIONS WERE RIGHT TO THE BYTE, WHICH IS THE CHANNEL RULE WORKING ===\n`PRETABLE2.out` named, before the run, the observable each new row would show under `M_MASK1`:\n  * `ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA_P2` -- predicted `0x77881122`, **measured `0x77881122`**;\n  * `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_P2` -- predicted `0x33440000`, **measured `0x33440000`**.\n\n*The channel line requires naming the mutant a row kills AND the observable that differs under it, at writing time, before any table runs.  Both were named and both were exact.*\n\n**ORDER PROVEN BY MTIME:** `PRETABLE2.out` 12:15:13, first table run 12:16:31 -- **78 s earlier**.  Written as a SEPARATE FILE rather than appended to `PRETABLE.out`, because this ledger records an append destroying a PRETABLE's mtime once, and the mtime is the artifact's entire evidentiary value.\n\n*** A PREDICTION OF MINE THAT MISSED, recorded because it is the same class I spent today cataloguing: I predicted `BASE 30/30` and the measurement is `29/29`. ***  I derived 30 from `grep -c 'record('` -- **a CORRELATE of the scored-row count, not the count itself** (one `record()` call is not scored).  *The deriver-keys-on-a-correlate rule, in my own pre-table prediction, on the same day I recorded it against three other censuses.  The error is harmless here because predictions and measurements are in separate sections and every citation names `SCORES.txt` -- which is exactly why they are kept apart.*\n\n**AND THUMB IS COVERED AFTER ALL, which settles part of an open question:** the failing-row lists name `THUMB_POP_WB_UNAL`, `THUMB_PUSH_WB_UNAL`, `THUMB_POP_COLD_DATA` and `THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA` -- so the instrument does exercise the Thumb callers, and `M_ENTRY` kills the Thumb writeback rows alongside the ARM ones.  *The `#524` pass-2 seat could not verify this because `cpu_arm.c` was outside its permitted set; the table settles it from the other direction.*\n\n**FLOOR STATUS FOR `#524`: (a) HEAD red, (b) reinstatement dies, (c) null medium 8/29, (d) `M_MASK1` KILLED at 27/29.  The breach is closed in-round under L1** -- nothing had shipped, so this is pre-ship construction rather than an L2 repair.  **Residue 3 disclosed in the contract rather than rowed**, because `~1` dies at `+2` and `~2` already dies at `+1`, so a `+3` row kills no enumerated mutant.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 TABLE 4 (`killtable_20260911d/`).  **TABLE 4: THE CLASS CLOSURE WORKS AND **BASE IS RED ON ONE OF MY OWN NEW ROWS** -- ROUND HELD.** ***\n\n=== THE REPAIR-CHAIN PASS FOUND A FLOOR BREACH AND REFUSED TO CLOSE THE CHAIN ===\n*VERDICT: FLOOR BREACH FOUND; the repair correctly detects `M_MASK1` but does not close the repair chain.*  **Two more clean-sweep candidates against the 29-row composite, both STATIC DEDUCTIONS rather than measured -- which is why each became a leg:**\n  * **`M_MASK7` (`& ~7`, OVER-alignment):** every base the instrument drove -- `0x10000`, `0x2000{1,2}`, `0x2100{1,2}` -- sits in the **FIRST WORD of its 8-byte block, where `~3` and `~7` AGREE.**  *And the two-register LDM does not rescue it: it asserts only `r0` writeback, never either loaded register.*\n  * **`M_PBITCOND` (a CONDITIONAL weakening on an EXISTING dominating predicate):** mask `~1` when `p_bit`, `~3` otherwise, at the STORE site only.  ARM STM is IA (`p_bit=0`, correct mask); Thumb PUSH is DB (`p_bit=1`, weakened) **but ran at exactly ONE residue, `+1`, where `~1` and `~3` agree.**  *The predicate axis was exercised at a single point.*\n\n**TWO CONSECUTIVE PASSES FOUND MASK-WEAKENING BREACHES, so the next repair owed CLASS CLOSURE rather than a third point fix.**  Space from the helpers' own inputs: **{site} x {residue} x {word position in the 8-byte block} x {p_bit}**.  Three uncovered cells closed in ONE diff.\n\n=== AND BOTH NEW MUTANTS DIED, ON THE CELLS WRITTEN FOR THEM ===\n\n| leg | score | killed on |\n|---|---|---|\n| `M_MASK7` | 30/32 | `ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA_BIT2`, `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2` |\n| `M_PBITCOND` | 30/32 | `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2`, `THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA_P2` |\n| `M_MASK1` | 28/32 | its two P2 rows **plus both new ones** |\n| `M_ENTRY` | 26/32 | the writeback rows |\n| `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | 8/32 | 21+ rows incl. both controls |\n| `HEAD` / `RESTORED` | 19/32 | identical -- clean restore against GIT |\n\n*** BUT `BASE: 31/32`.  THE FIX ITSELF FAILS `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2` -- one of the rows I ADDED THIS PASS -- and a red BASE row means the round DOES NOT SHIP. ***\n\n**The row reports `w0=0x00000000 w1=0x00000000` on the FIXED build.**  Expected: base `0x21005` masks to `0x21004`, so the word lands in `PAGE_STM+4`.  **Nothing landed in either word.**\n\n> **AND I COULD NOT MAKE THE ARITHMETIC CLOSE BY REASONING.**  Under HEAD the same row reports `w1=0x00000011`, which implies the stored bytes ENDED at `0x21004` -- i.e. a base of `0x21001`, not the `0x21005` my literal sets.  That contradicts `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA`, which calibrates correctly at base `0x21001` giving `0x22334400`.  *Two readings of the same mechanism that cannot both be right.*\n\n**SO I STOPPED REASONING AND INSTRUMENTED**, which is this project's own rule when a value is surprising: a diagnostic rebuilds BASE and **dumps a WIDE WINDOW around `PAGE_STM`, so the bytes can be LOCATED rather than guessed at.**  *I had been deriving where the store went without looking at where it actually is.*\n\n**TWO POSSIBILITIES, AND WHICH ONE IS NOT YET KNOWN:** my row is mis-specified -- most likely, since every other BASE row passes including `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_P2`, which uses the same mechanism at `+2` -- or it has found something real about the store path at bit-2-set addresses.  **Recorded as open rather than resolved in either direction.**\n\n*Also fixed this pass, from the panel: the mutator now emits **`0xfffffffc`, the EXACT shipped literal**, so `BASE` measures the working-tree text rather than a semantic equivalent -- grok raised that the table had not measured what would be committed.*\n\n*** DISCLOSED: THE PATH ROWS ARE FIXTURE CHECKS, NOT MUTATION DISCRIMINATORS, AND THE REPAIR PASS WAS RIGHT ABOUT THE ONES I ADDED. ***  Every `in_tlb_*` is captured BEFORE `run_prog`, so no helper-local mutation of a mask, operand or predicate can retroactively change it.  They establish the run was cold -- which the core comparison needs -- and discriminate NO enumerated mutant.  Kept for that purpose, disclosed rather than counted toward the floor.\n\n*And my `'a +3 row buys no enumerated mutant'` was TOO BROAD as a claim about the COMPOSITE: it holds within a fixed cell, but the missing distinction is the PREDICATE/RESIDUE COMBINATION, and residue 3 is one possible witness among several rather than one that buys nothing.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 TABLE 5 (`killtable_20260911e/`).  **THE RED BASE ROW WAS A DEFECT IN THE WITNESS, NOT THE FIX -- AND IT HAD CORRUPTED 8 OF 10 LEGS.  BASE IS NOW 32/32 AND THE FLOOR IS CLEAN.** ***\n\n=== HOW IT WAS FOUND, INCLUDING THE PART I DID BADLY ===\nTable 4 reported `BASE 31/32` -- the FIX failing `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2`.  **A red BASE row blocks the round, so it was diagnosed rather than explained away.**\n\n*** I FIRST TRIED TO REASON IT OUT FROM THE BYTE PATTERNS AND COULD NOT MAKE THE ARITHMETIC CLOSE. ***  Under HEAD the row implied the store ended at `0x21004` -- a base of `0x21001` -- while `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA` calibrates correctly at that same base.  **Two readings of one mechanism that cannot both be true**, and I spent four rounds of inference on it before instrumenting.\n\n**The dump settled it in ONE run:**\n    r0 = 0x21005                                 <- the literal loaded fine\n    0x00021000  00000000 44332211 00000000 ...   <- the store LANDED at 0x21004\n\n> **THE STORE WAS CORRECT ALL ALONG.  THE READ WAS WRONG.**\n\n=== THE INSTRUMENT DEFECT, MEASURED DIRECTLY ===\n    dump from an ALIGNED start:\n      0x00021000  00000000 44332211\n    dump from an UNALIGNED start -- what `read_mem32` actually issued:\n      0x00021000           44332211 00000000\n                  ^^^^^^^^ the leading group is BLANK-PADDED\n\n`read_mem32()` searched for the line labelled `(addr & ~0xF)` -- **which matches in BOTH cases** -- then indexed `line[12:48].split()` by `(addr & 0xF) // 4`.  **When the dump starts unaligned the leading groups are ABSENT and `.split()` collapses the padding, so every index shifts and `parts[idx]` silently returns a NEIGHBOURING WORD.**\n\n    read_mem32(0x21004) -> parts == ['44332211', '00000000'], idx 1 -> 0\n\n*This is the padded-column trap this project already records, in a new place: the label matched, so every check that looked for the right line passed, and the CONTENT came from the wrong column.*\n\n**REPAIR: dump from `(addr & ~0xF)` for 16 bytes, so all four groups are always present -- which is what the index arithmetic already assumed.  One line.**\n\n*** SCOPE, AND IT IS WIDER THAN THE ONE RED ROW: 8 OF 10 LEGS CHANGED VALUE WHEN THE INSTRUMENT WAS REPAIRED. ***\n\n| leg | table 4 | table 5 |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BASE` | 31/32 | **32/32** |\n| `HEAD` | 19/32 | **17/32** -- the pre-fix build was failing MORE rows than reported |\n| `RESTORED` | 19/32 | 17/32 (= HEAD) |\n| `M_ENTRY` | 26/32 | 27/32 |\n| `M_MASK1` | 28/32 | 29/32 |\n| `M_PBITCOND` | 30/32 | 31/32 |\n| `M_POP_ONLY` | 25/32 | 23/32 |\n| `M_PUSH_ONLY` | 25/32 | 26/32 |\n| `M_MASK7`, `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | unchanged | unchanged |\n\n**The defect was in the probe AS ORIGINALLY BUILT**, so it affected every `read_mem32` call at a nonzero 16-byte offset -- the STM and Thumb PUSH clobber rows -- across tables 1 through 4.  **Every number those rows produced before this table is superseded.**  *Grepped: no other probe in the tree shares the shape, so the blast radius is this round's instrument alone.*\n\n=== THE FLOOR, NOW CLEAN ===\n\n| clause | leg | score | killed on |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| -- | `BASE` | **32/32** | **nothing -- clean** |\n| (a) pre-fix red | `HEAD` | 17/32 | the defect rows |\n| (b) reinstatement | `RESTORED` | 17/32 | = HEAD exactly |\n| (c) null medium | `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | 8/32 | 24 rows |\n| **(d)** | `M_MASK1` | 29/32 | the three `P2` rows |\n| **(d)** | `M_MASK7` | 30/32 | the two `BIT2` rows |\n| **(d)** | `M_PBITCOND` | 31/32 | **`THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA_P2` alone -- the minimum** |\n| de-escalation | `M_ENTRY` | 27/32 | **all five writeback rows, nothing else** |\n\n**Every mutant dies on exactly the cells written for it, and `M_PBITCOND` on precisely one.**  *The class closure holds under a correct instrument, which is the thing table 4 could not establish.*\n\n*A small one of mine, recorded because I spent today cataloguing this class: my own shell check for a clean BASE tested `[ -z \"$B\" ]`, but the probe prints `FAILING: none` when clean -- a NON-EMPTY string -- so the check took its else branch and printed 'BASE still red on: none'.  The score `32/32` is what settled it.  A check that reports the opposite of the truth on a green run is the same family as the ones I filed against other people's scripts today.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PROMOTED TO regress/ AND WIRED INTO gate_arm.sh -- 32/32 AGAINST THE GATE'S OWN BINARY. ***\n\n**This discharges the obligation the round could not previously claim.**  Fable's adjudication found that *neither of today's probes is under `regress/` proper, so 'shipped a detector with the fix' is not yet true of either round.*  For `#524` it is true now.\n\n=== THE PROMOTION IS A COPY, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE ===\n`regress/artifacts/armbdt/agy_armbdt_witness_20260911.py` **stays in place as the AS-RUN record of tables 1-5**; `regress/arm_bdt_probe.py` is the copy that evolves and that the gate executes.  *Editing the artifact copy would falsify what those tables actually measured* -- the same reason the repaired kill-table driver was written as a new file rather than over the old one.\n\n**The promoted copy differs only by two PRINT-ONLY lines (`ARMBDT_CONTROL=`, `ARMBDT_RESULT=`, matching the gate idiom the other ARM probes use) and a header note.  Verified: the `record()` count is unchanged, so no row was added or lost in the copy.**  Its header also carries the `read_mem32` alignment warning, so the next probe copied from it does not inherit [[probereadalign]].\n\n=== MEASURED AGAINST THE GATE'S OWN BINARY, NOT THE SCRATCH CLONE ===\n`$PMAX` is `build/gxemul`.  After the propagate+rebuild, the promoted probe run directly against it returns **`SCORE 32/32`, `ARMBDT_CONTROL=OK`, `ARMBDT_RESULT=32/32`, `FAILING: none`**.  *A pre-check worth the thirty seconds: wiring the probe in BEFORE the rebuild would have run it against a pre-fix binary and produced a red gate that meant nothing about the fix.*\n\n=== THE GATE SECTION, AND WHY THE DENOMINATOR IS A LITERAL ===\nFollows `arm_writeback_probe.py`'s shape exactly: run -> `gate_skip` if no result line -> print the ok/FAIL rows -> check the control -> **`check \"bdt rows run\" \"$bwant\" 32`** -> check correct == run.\n\n> **The denominator is pinned to a LITERAL deliberately.  The probe grew 25 -> 29 -> 32 rows across three kill tables as clause-(d) mutants were closed, and a gate that trusts the probe's own denominator cannot tell a probe that LOST rows from one that never had them.**\n\nThe section also records all five measured-dead mutants with their scores and the row family that kills each (`M_ENTRY` 27/32 writeback rows, `M_MASK1` 29/32 the `+2` rows, `M_MASK7` 30/32 the bit-2 rows, `M_PBITCOND` 31/32 Thumb PUSH `+2`, `M_NULL_MEDIUM` 8/32), **so a future reader meets the evidence at the gate rather than having to find the artifacts.**\n\n*Targeted gate run in flight.  Nothing committed; the owner's hold stands until it is green.*",
43811:   "note": "FILED 2026-09-10 from the GPT-6 Astra exit audit (regress/artifacts/queuetriage/codex_exitaudit_20260910.txt), which ranked it I1/E1. VERIFIED LOCALLY by opening the file before filing.\n\nThe read arm of `case 0x04: /* MAPLE_DMAADDR */` reads, in order:\n    fatal(\"[ dreamcast_maple: TODO: read from dmaaddr ]\\n\");\n    odata = d->dmaaddr;\n    exit(1);\n**The correct value is computed and then thrown away by terminating the process.**  The WRITE arm two lines above stores `d->dmaaddr = idata', so the register is FULLY MODELLED in both directions -- a guest that writes the DMA address and reads it back, which is the ordinary way to confirm a controller programming step, is killed on the read.\n\nREACHABILITY: machine_dreamcast.c adds `dreamcast_maple' unconditionally.  Nothing has to start DMA or build a command list; the plain readback reaches it.\n\n*** THIS IS THE ACCEPT MEMBER'S TEXTBOOK SHAPE, which the option set records as member 4: the model ALREADY computes the right value and then discards it, so the disposition is to delete the kill and KEEP THE VALUE, with NO latch -- there is no event to announce, and a bounded announce would ship a wrong record in product code (an `unimplemented; ignored' message about a read that is implemented and is not ignored). ***  Two deleted lines.\n\nFAULT is not available and would be wrong anyway: memory_rw.c converts a failed device callback into an architectural access exception for MIPS and M88K ONLY, and this device sits on an SH4 machine -- but the access is implemented, so refusing it would be the wrong answer even if the machinery existed.\n\nTEST CARRIER EXISTS: regress/gdrom_default_probe.py already supplies a Dreamcast session with guest load/store helpers.  The oracle must assert that a separately STORED value reads back EXACTLY and that execution continues -- observing a zero, or a surviving debugger prompt, would be a vacuous row, since both are true of several wrong implementations.\n\nNOT MEASURED and no witness built.  The rung-3 attempt is owed before this may be recorded documented-only.\n\n*** DESIGN REVIEW 2026-09-10 (acceptclass/codex_acceptclass_20260910.txt): VERDICT IS **HOLD** THE TWO-LINE ACCEPT, AND IT REFUTED A PREMISE OF MINE.  Both points verified locally before recording. ***\n\n**MY PREMISE 'the correct value is computed and then thrown away' IS TOO GENEROUS.**  `odata` is a LOCAL.  The copy that delivers it -- `memory_writemax64(cpu, data, len, odata)' -- sits at the FUNCTION TAIL, after the switch, so `exit(1)' fires first and **the value never reaches the caller at all**.  The fix is unchanged (deleting the kill lets execution reach the tail and the value IS then delivered), but the DESCRIPTION was wrong and a wrong description gets reused.\n\n**AND THE ROUND IS HELD ON A SEMANTIC QUESTION I DID NOT ASK: what should a read of the DMA address RETURN?**  Real Maple hardware may advance a DMA cursor, in which case a post-transfer readback should give the ADVANCED cursor rather than the address the guest programmed.  The model stores only what was written.  So ACCEPT-as-stored may be shipping a plausible wrong value in place of a loud stop, which is the worse outcome and is exactly the hazard the brief's own question 2 raised about the `TODO'.  **The round owes a settled hardware contract before it may ship**, and the detector's `A.afterdma' row must take its expected value FROM THAT CONTRACT rather than from today's implementation -- with transfer evidence alongside the address readback, so that skipping DMA cannot manufacture a pass.\n\n*** THE HELD QUESTION WAS AIMED AT THE WRONG ARM, AND THE TREE ANSWERS IT ITSELF.  Found 2026-09-10 by opening the file, in under two minutes, which is what the check-do-not-judge rule is for. ***\n\nThe review held this round on *what should a read of the DMA address RETURN -- the programmed address, or an ADVANCED cursor?*, and warned that ACCEPT-as-stored might ship a plausible wrong value in place of a loud stop.  **The premise under that question is that the model stores only what was written.  IT DOES NOT.**\n\n`maple_do_dma_xfer()' walks the command list accumulating `addr += datalen_cmd * 4' and then, at its tail, immediately before asserting SYSASIC_EVENT_MAPLE_DMADONE:\n\n        // TODO: Should the DMA address be updated?\n        d->dmaaddr = addr;\n\n**So the advancing cursor is ALREADY MODELLED, and the tree flags its own uncertainty about it in a comment one line above.**  A read after a transfer would return the ADVANCED address; a read before any transfer returns the programmed one.\n\n*** WHAT THAT CHANGES: THE TWO QUESTIONS ARE ON DIFFERENT SITES AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS THIS ROUND'S. ***  The read arm does not decide what `d->dmaaddr' holds -- it is a pure reporter of a field that `maple_do_dma_xfer()' owns.  Whether the advance is architecturally right is a PRE-EXISTING open question at a DIFFERENT site, which this round neither creates nor resolves, and deleting the read-side termination leaves it exactly where it was.  **Filed separately as [[mapledmaadvance]] rather than folded in here** -- the same discipline the sibling review applied to [[xscaleinten]], where a write-side kill on the adjacent case was ruled a separate row.\n\n**AND THE `plausible wrong value versus a loud stop' TRADE IS WEAKER THAN IT LOOKS, in two ways worth stating rather than glossing.**  The loud stop is `exit(1)' -- it ends the emulator, so nobody acts on it as a diagnostic; and the SAME unverified address is ALREADY handed to the DMA engine on every transfer, so the exposure the review is protecting against exists whether or not a guest ever reads the register back.  The read arm is the one place the value is currently NOT consumed.\n\n**THE DETECTOR CONSEQUENCE, which is the part the review was right to press on:** the after-DMA row must NOT pin the advanced value as architecturally correct, because this round has not established that.  It pins the READ ARM'S contract, which is what the diff changes -- (i) a stored value reads back exactly with no transfer in between, and (ii) the value read after a transfer DIFFERS from the programmed one, which proves the read reports the live field rather than a constant or a stale snapshot -- with transfer evidence alongside, so that a skipped DMA cannot manufacture a pass.  The advanced value's CORRECTNESS is [[mapledmaadvance]]'s to pin, and the contract paragraph says so under clause (e).\n\n*Recorded before any seat was asked, then sent for checking rather than acted on: the review that raised the hold was right that the round owed an answer, and the answer turned out to be in the file rather than in a datasheet.*\n\n*** REVIEWED 2026-09-10 (mapledmaaddr/codex_readarm_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, ZERO cyber flags).  VERDICT: **THE READ-ARM CORRECTION MAY PROCEED INDEPENDENTLY.**  My state trace was right, my observability claim was wrong, and MY PROPOSED DETECTOR IS INSUFFICIENT. ***\n\n**THE SEPARATION HOLDS**, on a bounded basis the review stated rather than assumed: the read correction implements a separately testable rule -- *return the stored register field* -- while the cursor item decides what the transfer routine should STORE there.  The cursor policy already affects execution beyond this arm, since a subsequent transfer starts from `d->dmaaddr' and so already consumes the advanced value whether or not any guest reads it.\n\n**BUT MY FRAMING WAS TOO STRONG IN ONE PLACE, and the correction is exact:** *\"the edit does not change cursor computation\"* is correct; *\"the edit does not change the OBSERVABILITY of that computation\"* would NOT be -- a guest read that previously stopped execution can now obtain the field.  That is a new observable, and the round must say so rather than claim the diff is behaviour-preserving.\n\nTHREE CORRECTIONS TO MY OWN NOTE ABOVE, all from reading the file:\n  1. **The advance is MORE than the payload increment I quoted.**  Each descriptor advances by **8 bytes of control words + 4 bytes of command word + 4*datalen_cmd of data**, so the committed value is `C = (P + SUM(12 + 4*D_i)) mod 2^32'.  I wrote only the `4 * datalen_cmd' term.\n  2. **\"Advanced\" does not mean numerically larger** -- the sum is taken modulo 2^32, so wraparound is possible, and it is not guaranteed that `C != P'.\n  3. *** MY EARLY-EXIT CLAIM WAS WRONG. ***  I wrote that after an aborted transfer the field holds *the PROGRAMMED address*.  It holds the **ENTRY value**, which may itself be a PREVIOUS transfer's cursor -- an early return preserves what was there, it does not restore a separately retained programmed address.  Also the `!d->enable' check comes FIRST, so a disabled AND unaligned address takes the disabled return and never reaches the alignment diagnostic.\n\nWRITERS OF THE FIELD, complete for these two files: structure initialisation, the register write arm, and the DMA completion assignment.  Neither reset register clears it (`MAPLE_RESET' clears `enable'), and the tick function touches input queues only.  **The cursor is published at the TAIL, not incrementally**, and the ordinary trigger is SYNCHRONOUS -- writing 1 to `MAPLE_STATE' calls the transfer routine directly, with no scheduled DMA worker -- so no partially-advanced value can be observed.  *Scope caveat the seat raised itself: the routine calls `cpu->memory_rw', and these two files do not establish that such a callback can never re-enter device access.*\n\n*** THE DETECTOR I PROPOSED IS INSUFFICIENT, AND THIS IS THE PART THAT CHANGES THE ROUND. ***\n  * **Assertion (i), exact readback with no DMA, is passable by four wrong implementations:** a constant equal to the one test value; constant zero against an initial-zero-only test; ALIGNMENT MASKING against any test using only aligned values; and a separate snapshot of the last programmed address that never tracks DMA at all.  *Remedy: several distinct values, including nonzero HIGH bits and nonzero LOW FIVE bits, and successive writes shown to replace what reads report.  No DMA is needed for the unaligned cases.*\n  * **Assertion (ii), post-transfer inequality, proves only that the answer differs from `P'** -- satisfied by zero, by a fabricated `P + 4', by a stale cursor from an earlier transfer, and by any masked transform that still differs.  Independent DMA evidence rules out a SKIPPED transfer; it says nothing about the relationship between the returned value and the field.\n  * **THE FORM THAT WORKS:** capture the actual field value `S' immediately before the read, then assert the delivered register value equals `S' EXACTLY, that the read leaves the field unchanged, and that the access returns normally with NO read-side diagnostic.  Apply it both after ordinary programming and after an independently demonstrated transfer.\n  * *** `MAPLE_STATE == 0' IS NOT DMA EVIDENCE: its read arm always returns zero, including after an early return. ***  Use a supported command and verify a fresh expected response in a receive buffer pre-filled with distinguishable contents; a completion event alone is weaker, because the descriptor loop can reach the tail through paths that do not implement the request fully.\n  * **Candidate (c) -- keep `fatal()' and delete only `exit(1)' -- is NOT rejected by any value assertion if `fatal()' returns.  Silence and normal completion must be checked separately.**\n\nPARTITION OF THE CHANGE, exact: only `relative_addr == 0x04' in the non-write branch moves.  The changed read class includes EVERY field value and history -- initial zero, freshly programmed values, UNALIGNED values, completed-DMA cursors, values preserved by disabled or unaligned attempts, and values retained across reset writes -- with no enable or alignment condition on the corrected read.  *A syntactic subtlety worth a row's comment: the `else' also covers a direction flag equal to neither MEM_WRITE nor MEM_READ, which previously terminated and afterwards assigns `odata' and returns without serialising it.*\n\nAttachment is unconditional at `machine_dreamcast.c:149', BEFORE the `prom_emulation' early return.\n\n*** A 'SETTLED PREMISE' I GAVE THE PANEL WAS A FACT ABOUT A DIFFERENT DEVICE.  Corrected 2026-09-11 (mapledmaaddr/codex_detector_20260911.txt), verified here by opening the file. ***\n\nI wrote, as settled: *\"`odata` is computed BEFORE the offset is examined, and delivered by the common tail, so an 'ignored' read still returns the value.\"*  **That is true of `dev_wdc.c`'s alt-status handler and FALSE of this one.**  `dev_dreamcast_maple.c` initialises `odata = 0` at `:550`, assigns `odata = d->dmaaddr` **INSIDE** the `0x04` read arm, and delivers at `:638`.\n\n**THE CONSEQUENCE IS NOT COSMETIC: for Maple an ignored read returns ZERO, not the field.**  So the *decline-in-place* option here is **not** equivalent to *change nothing*, which is exactly what it WAS for the WDC row -- and I had carried that equivalence across as though it were a property of the pattern rather than of that file.\n\n*** THIS IS THE FOURTH SCOPE-TRANSFER ERROR OF THE SESSION AND THEY ARE ALL ONE SHAPE: A CLAIM VERIFIED IN ONE SCOPE, REUSED IN ANOTHER WITHOUT RE-CHECKING. ***\n  1. `#519`: the clause-(d) flow test applied correctly to `tlb_mode`, then **stopped at the array and never reached the index** -- six escapes, round re-opened.\n  2. `#519`: the `color_mode` warrant true of the arm I edited, **false of its sibling arms**.\n  3. `rlwinm`: `rlwimi`'s ordering warrant **did not transfer one call earlier**, into the decoder -- the adopted repair was inert.\n  4. here: a WDC handler's control flow **transferred to a Maple handler** that does not share it.\n\n**Each was caught by a seat or by reading, never by re-reading my own reasoning** -- which is the argument for the premise-audit opener and for naming premises as QUESTIONS rather than as settled facts.  *A premise inherited from a sibling investigation is the most dangerous kind, because it arrives already believed.*\n\n**THE DETECTOR IS VIABLE: it can distinguish all six states**, provided the read-value, DMA-response, execution and diagnostic assertions are kept **separate** rather than folded into one row.  The strict oracle is *the guest's returned 32-bit value equals an INDEPENDENTLY OBSERVED current `d->dmaaddr`* -- obtained by host-side inspection immediately before the read, and **not derived from the same path under test.**\n\n*** 2026-09-11: A SEVENTH CAUSE OF A ZERO-BYTE CODEX ANSWER: TRANSPORT, AND IT DEMANDS THE OPPOSITE RESPONSE FROM THE ONE THE RECORD WOULD HAVE GIVEN IT. ***\n\n`regress/artifacts/mapledmaaddr/codex_detector_20260911.txt` is ZERO BYTES with a 213 KB `.err`.  The staging review was about to file it under the two-refusals rule -- *\"if it refuses twice, commit both zero-byte files as the record and stop\"*.  **It never refused.**  Its `.err` ends:\n\n    ERROR: Reconnecting... 2/5\n    ERROR: Reconnecting... 3/5\n    ERROR: Reconnecting... 4/5\n\nand the content immediately before that is ordinary substantive analysis, mid-sentence, of the probe's `dma alias` row.  **The seat was answering and the connection died under it.**\n\nTHE SIX CAUSES ALREADY ON RECORD -- terminal refusal, quota wall, still in flight, cp1252 decode death, auth revocation, and `rc=-1` with the answer in the `.err` -- **none fits.**  There is no cyber flag at the tail, no quota text, the process exited, the decode is fine, auth is live (other codex jobs answered in the same window), and `rc` was 0.\n\n> **THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE `.err` TAIL: `Reconnecting... N/5` MEANS TRANSPORT, AND A TRANSPORT FAILURE IS RE-FIRED IMMEDIATELY.**  A refusal is the seat declining the SUBJECT, so re-firing the same brief buys nothing and the two-refusals rule exists to stop you paying twice for it.  A transport drop is content-independent -- *the seat had no opinion about the brief at all* -- so re-firing is the correct and only response.\n\n**WHY THE MISFILING WOULD HAVE BEEN EXPENSIVE RATHER THAN UNTIDY:** filing it as a refusal would have recorded *\"codex declines the Maple subject\"* -- a durable, false fact about a SEAT, of exactly the kind this project has repeatedly had to un-learn (\"it was never the seat, it was the brief\", the kimi substitute left unfired for days, minimax written off for a week).  **A seat wrongly recorded as refusing a subject is a seat nobody fires again.**\n\n*This is the daemon-rule's shape one transport up: a WALL is the seat refusing and you wait it out; a REFUSED CONNECTION is the transport being absent and one command fixes it.  Codex has now shown the second kind, and its `.err` says so in as many words -- which is why the standing rule is CHECK THE `.err`, NOT THE SIZE.*\n\nRe-fired as `codex_detector_RETRY_20260911`.  **Both files are kept**: 21 zero-byte codex answers are already tracked, all `codex_*`, including a committed failed-retry PAIR at `crimewidthkill/codex_witness_20260910.txt` + `..._RETRY_...`.  The zero-byte file IS the record of the loss, and now carries its cause.\n\n*** THE TRANSPORT CLASS ABOVE IS REFUTED BY ITS OWN INSTANCE.  I WROTE IT HOURS AGO AND THE ANSWER LANDED COMPLETE. ***\n\n`codex_detector_20260911.txt` is **16,624 bytes**, carries its liveness token, and ends in a finished sentence.  **It was never lost.**  And the `Reconnecting... N/5` lines I keyed on sit at `.err` lines **3821-3823 of 3976** -- *155 lines from the end, not at the tail*.  The CLI reconnected and carried on.\n\n**SO THE DIAGNOSIS WAS EXACTLY BACKWARDS: the seat had an opinion about the brief, delivered it, and I recorded that it had none.**  The zero bytes I read were the NORMAL MID-RUN STATE of a direct `codex exec` -- whose shell creates the redirect target at launch, so **0 bytes means started-and-running**, which is the discriminator I had written into `codexfarm.py` myself earlier the same session.\n\n> *** THIS IS THE SECOND TIME TODAY I READ A MID-RUN FILE AS A DEAD JOB, AND THE FIRST TIME COST A COMPLETED 12,032-BYTE ANSWER. ***  The structural fix I built covers `codexfarm` jobs, which now carry a `.RUNNING` sentinel.  **This job was a direct `codex exec` with a shell redirect and has no sentinel** -- so the fix did not reach the case, and I applied the wrong rule again to the one shape it does not cover.\n\n**WHAT SURVIVES, and it is narrow:** a genuinely dead transport is still distinguishable, and the `codexfarm.py` check I added tests the **LAST EIGHT LINES** plus an empty answer.  Here the reconnects are 155 lines from the end, so **that check would correctly NOT have fired** -- it is narrower than the prose I wrote around it, and the prose was the defective half.\n\n**THE CORRECTED RULE: `Reconnecting... N/5` ANYWHERE IN A `.err` MEANS THE CLI HIT NETWORK TROUBLE, NOT THAT THE JOB DIED.**  It is diagnostic of nothing on its own.  What decides liveness is the same thing it always was: **the job has not notified yet** -- the harness tracks background jobs and reports on exit, and that signal was available and unconsulted both times.\n\n*A correction is not safer than the thing it corrects: this one minted a class from a single instance, exactly as this project's own freshly-minted-exemplar rule warns, and the instance refuted it four minutes later.  The retry fired at a DIFFERENT path, so nothing was clobbered -- the path discipline held where the reasoning did not, and the retry is kept as an independent second answer on one brief rather than cancelled.*\n\n**THE ANSWER ITSELF REPORTS A FINDING WORTH THE ROUND:** *\"A detector can separate the six states, but one 'settled' premise is false in the specified source.\"*  Unharvested; that is the next step on this row.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- **CORRECTING THE TEMPLATE DOES NOT CORRECT THE BRIEFS ALREADY ON DISK**, AND I FIRED THE UNCORRECTED ONE TWICE TODAY. ***\n\nThe brief's settled-premises block said *\"`odata` is computed BEFORE the offset is examined, and delivered by the common tail (`if (writeflag == MEM_READ) data[0] = odata;`), so an 'ignored' read still returns the value.\"*  **True of `dev_wdc.c`.  FALSE of this device.**\n\n`dev_dreamcast_maple.c:550` initialises `uint64_t idata = 0, odata = 0;`; the `0x04` arm assigns inside itself; the value returns through `memory_writemax64()` at `:637`.  **There is no `data[0] = odata` tail here, so deleting the assignment returns ZERO rather than the live value** -- which inverts what the oracle must look for.  Verified by opening the file.\n\n*** THE CHAIN IS THE FINDING, AND IT IS NEW. ***\n  1. The error was made -- a premise carried from `dev_wdc.c` to a device that does not share it.\n  2. It was CAUGHT, and recorded as **instance #4 in `tools/BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md`'s scope-transfer table (`:253`), naming this exact device and this exact premise.**\n  3. **The BRIEF was never corrected.**\n  4. I fired that brief today.  Twice -- the original and a retry.\n  5. The seat caught the same error again, independently, from the source.\n\n> **A TEMPLATE GOVERNS BRIEFS WRITTEN *FROM* IT.  IT DOES NOT REACH BRIEFS ALREADY WRITTEN.**  Recording a scope-transfer instance in the template closes the class for FUTURE briefs and leaves the originating brief on disk, loaded, refutable, and re-fireable.\n\n*This is the carrier's own thesis landing on the one artifact it holds up as the piece of prose that demonstrably works: the premise-audit block fires because it lives where the decision happens.  It does -- for a brief being WRITTEN.  The decision to RE-FIRE an existing brief happens somewhere else entirely, and nothing was watching there.*\n\n**REMEDY APPLIED: the brief is corrected IN PLACE, quoting what it refutes** (the retry now running had already read the old text, so this stops the third firing rather than the second).  **The general form -- when a scope-transfer instance is recorded in the template, correct the brief it came from in the same edit -- is a one-line habit and belongs in the template's own §C block.**\n\nAND THE ANSWER'S DESIGN CONTENT STANDS, with four vacuity findings stated against NAMED mutants rather than universally: post-transfer inequality is **insufficient** (many wrong values satisfy it); the unaligned-disabled retry is **vacuous for enable-guard deletion** (alignment refusal preserves the same entry value); **silence alone is vacuous** (no execution also produces no diagnostic); and a fresh expected response is **vacuous for a read-arm-only value mutant** (it establishes DMA execution; the address row carries the value rejection).  Weakenings: (a) DIFFERENT, (c) DIFFERENT, (d) DIFFERENT, and **(b) EQUIVALENT for the `0x04` contract but DIFFERENT for the whole callback** -- `W(04,1); R(10)` returns 0 originally and 1 after the hoist, caught by an `other zero` row.\n\n*Method note: my first grep for the premise in the brief returned ZERO and I nearly reported the brief clean -- the text is `` `odata` is computed `` and the backticks broke the pattern.  `grep -F` with a denominator found it at lines 39-40.  The pattern-that-matched-nothing trap, inside the check for a different error.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- **THE RETRY REPLICATES THE REFUTATION INDEPENDENTLY (badge `s`)**. ***\n\nThe re-fire (18,024 b, `codex_detector_RETRY_20260911.txt`) reaches the same verdict from the same three files in a separate invocation: *\"The two-line fix is sound.  A strong detector can compare readback with an independent observation of the live field, without endorsing the cursor arithmetic.  **One 'settled' premise is false in this checkout.**\"*\n\n**So my `odata` premise is refuted TWICE, independently.**  Badged `s` -- SAME-MODEL replication, which this project's doctrine ranks below two-family agreement and forbids passing off as it.  *It is still worth more than one answer: the two runs did not share a process, and the second was fired before I had corrected the brief, so both read the identical defective packet and both caught it.*\n\n**AND THE RETRY ANSWERS THE QUESTION I MOST WANTED:** the detector can pin the read arm's contract **by comparing readback against an INDEPENDENT observation of the live field**, *\"without endorsing the cursor arithmetic\"* -- i.e. without certifying that the advanced value is architecturally right, which is a separate open question with no local documentation.  That is exactly the separation the contract paragraph needs.\n\n*The duplicate firing was caused by my own misdiagnosis and cost one extra invocation.  Recorded honestly: it bought a replication, but it was not bought deliberately, and a replication chosen on purpose would have gone to a DIFFERENT family.*",
44285:   "id": "xscaleinten",
44290:   "note": "*** 2026-09-12, codex farm: the first brief was REFUSED by the codex cybersecurity filter; re-worded as plain mutation testing it answered (regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/codex_promote_20260912b.txt).  regress/xscale_inten_probe.py CREATED from the witness + 16 codex replacements (D_OP1, INTEN_WRITE_PRESERVES_ISTEER, BLOCKED reporting, stale-prompt and ignored-timeout fixes, RESULT/CONTROL lines) + D_CRM1_TMR0, the crm-isolation row codex said remained open (tmr0 <- 0x5A5A0000, TMRx_ENABLE clear).  29 rows, UNMEASURED.  The gate_arm.sh section is designed, NOT applied.  MANIFEST entry owed. ***\n\n*** PASS 2, 2026-09-11 (GPT-6 Astra, effort max): **A CLAUSE-(d) FLOOR BREACH IS PREDICTED, NOT YET\nMEASURED.**  THE ROUND IS NOT SHIPPABLE UNTIL IT IS BUILT AND SCORED. ***  Cited: regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/codex_pass2_20260911.txt\n\nVerdict: *\"The detector has holes: an opcode1-gated reinstatement escapes, and several recorded\nkills are misattributed. I found no reason to change the deletion itself.\"*\n\n**THE ESCAPING MUTANT (`M_OPCODE1`), inside the existing inten load arm, after the assignment:**\n    if (opcode1 != 0) { fatal(\"TODO: XScale read from inten?\\n\"); exit(1); }\n\nIt keeps the enclosing predicates and introduces a DIFFERENT one, which is exactly the clause-(d)\nform a PURE DELETION owes -- not the original death condition widened.  **The seat DECODED THE\nLITERAL CP6 INSTRUCTION WORDS in the probe** and found every tested inten access carries\n`opcode1 == 0`; the only nonzero-opcode1 instruction is `E_UNKNOWN` (`opcode1=3, crn=9, crm=2`),\nwhich never reaches the edited arm and dies through `unknown` anyway.  So it predicts **26/26, a\nTOTAL ESCAPE**, while a guest issuing an inten read with nonzero opcode1 would be killed -- so it is\nNOT an equivalent mutant.\n\n*** THAT 26/26 IS A PREDICTION WRITTEN IN THE MEASURED COLUMN'S CHAIR, AND THIS PROJECT HAS PAID\nFOR THAT FOUR TIMES.  IT IS RECORDED AS OWED, NOT AS A SCORE. ***  The leg must be BUILT and RUN\nbefore the breach is graded L2 -- deferred only because a gate held the build trees when it was\nfound.  If it escapes, it is **L2: replace IN-ROUND however late**.\n\n**THE PRETABLE'S clause-(e) DISCLOSURE DOES NOT COVER IT.**  It discloses `opcode1=3` as *\"tested in\narm E\"* -- but arm E tests that value in an ALREADY-REJECTED register tuple, which says nothing\nabout opcode1 at `crm=0, crn=0`.  **A disclosure that names the right VALUE at the wrong SITE is not\na disclosure of the cell.**  The PRETABLE's GUARD FIELD list is also wrong to call opcode1/opcode2\nguards: they are function INPUTS that this function never dispatches on.\n\nTWO MORE ROWS OWED, both cheap and both from the same pass:\n  * **`INTEN_WRITE_PRESERVES_ISTEER`** -- write isteer=0, write inten=S1, read isteer back and\n    expect 0.  A SECOND anchored row against the M5 register substitution, independent of the\n    free-running IRQ path.  **Today `ANCHOR_EXC` is the SOLE discriminator for M5 (25/26) and it is\n    the suite's most load-sensitive row** -- breakpoint plus timeout plus free-run.\n  * **a stepped `tmr0` read at `crm=1, crn=0, l_bit=1`** -- the de-escalation accept row for the\n    `crm` predicate IN ISOLATION.  `D_TRR0` changes crm AND crn together, so it does not hold the\n    other former gates true.  tmr0 is chosen because its read has none of tcr0/tcr1's\n    counter-increment side effect.\n\nMY M5 READING WAS UPHELD WITH TWO CORRECTIONS WORTH KEEPING: **not every `D_*` row is a round-trip**\n(`D_RESET` reads before any inten write and `D_TRR0` exercises another register -- `D_RESET` merely\nfails to discriminate because the reset values coincide), and *\"one discriminator for this\nconsistent M5 substitution\"* is NARROWER than *\"one row capable of detecting any wrong-register\nedit\"*.  *A locally-true restatement that widens a claim is the shape this project has recorded as\nharder to catch than a plain wrong answer.*\n\nTHE FIX ITSELF IS UNCHANGED AND ENDORSED: *\"I would keep the fix exactly as supplied\"*, and ACCEPT\n(option-set member 4) is upheld -- *\"I cannot justify a once-only announcement for the implemented\nread.\"*  **Eight passes on this round's detector, none on its fix until now, and the first pass on\nthe fix approved it while finding the detector's enumeration incomplete.**\n\n---\n\n*** 2026-09-11 L4 RECORD CORRECTION -- THIS ROW READ `state: open' WITH NO WITNESS FIELD WHILE A\nTWO-COLOUR RUNG-3 WITNESS, A PRETABLE.out AND A GREEN SEVEN-LEG KILL TABLE SAT IN ITS OWN\nARTIFACTS DIRECTORY. ***\n\nI wrote `xscale -- no round run' into the checkpoint this afternoon and it was FALSE.  Found by\nopening `regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/', not by any check.  What is measured there:\n\n    BASE       26/26  FAILING: NONE          M_OPCODE2  19/26  (7 D_* rows red)\n    HEAD       11/26  (15 rows red)          M_RD15     25/26  (RD15_SURVIVE)\n    M1         23/26  (3 ANCHOR rows red)    M_NREAD    19/26  (7 D_* rows red)\n    M5         25/26  (ANCHOR_EXC)           RESTORED   26/26  FAILING: NONE\n\ncited: regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/killtable_20260911/SCORES.txt.  **Floor clause (a) is met\n(HEAD red), every mutant is dead on its intended rows, and the restore verified BOTH parts --\nsource cmp-vs-SEC CLEAN and binary FRESH -- before clearing the sentinel.**\n\nTHE DENOMINATOR, because the first draft of this correction overstated the class and the count\nrefuted it: 169 ledger rows sit in work/held/open with zero entries, which is the NORMAL state of\na filed row.  Testing each for an artifacts dir holding a witness or killtable narrows it to\n**FOUR** -- ppcrldimimask, ppcrlwimiwidth, wdcaltdigin and this row -- and the other three are\nrecorded CORRECTLY, `in work' with a witness field naming the artifact.  *The records practice is\nholding; this row was the sole outlier, and a systemic claim would have been wrong.*\n\nSTILL OWED and NOT discharged by this edit: the round has had THREE seats (codex x2 2026-09-10,\nagy x1 2026-09-11) against a standing NINE-SEAT directive, and no panel dir cites it.  The fix is\nuncommitted (` M src/cpus/cpu_arm_coproc.c'), byte-identical in est/, p1=CLEAN p2=FRESH in all\nthree build trees.  **HELD for the panel under the owner's hold-everything ruling -- this edit\ncorrects the RECORD only and licenses nothing.**\n\n---\n\nFILED 2026-09-10 from the GPT-6 Astra exit audit (regress/artifacts/queuetriage/codex_exitaudit_20260910.txt), which ranked it I1/E1. VERIFIED LOCALLY by opening the file before filing.\n\nIn `arm_coproc_i80321_6', crm 0 / crn 0, the load arm reads:\n    cpu->cd.arm.r[rd] = cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten;\n    fatal(\"TODO: XScale read from inten?\\n\");\n    exit(1);\nThe destination register receives the modelled value and the process then dies.  The STORE arm immediately below writes the same field, and the i80321 interrupt controller consumes it, so the register is fully modelled in both directions.\n\n*** THE DECISIVE EVIDENCE IS THE SIBLING CASE IN THE SAME SWITCH, and it is one screen away: `case 4:' (i80321_isteer) has the IDENTICAL structure -- load arm assigns `cpu->cd.arm.r[rd] = cpu->cd.arm.i80321_isteer;', store arm writes it -- and carries NO fatal() and NO exit(). ***  So crn 0's kill is an OUTLIER inside its own switch rather than a policy, which is the upstream-residue pattern this project has now found several times: the author hit the exit he happened to reach while developing, and the sibling he did not.  That is a far stronger warrant than 'it is a host kill', and it is one grep away -- exactly what the option-set rule's sibling check is for.\n\nREACHABILITY: machine_iq80321.c installs it explicitly (`cpu->cd.arm.coproc[6] = arm_coproc_i80321_6;'), and Iyonix does the same.  This is not an unattached helper. Emulated firmware reading its own interrupt-enable register is the natural consumer, and the generic coprocessor dispatcher enforces no supervisor-only precondition of its own.\n\nDISPOSITION: option-set member 4 (ACCEPT) -- delete the two lines, keep the assignment, with NO latch, for the same reason as [[mapledmaaddr]]: the read is implemented and is not ignored, so an announce would ship a wrong record.  FAULT is unavailable here in any case: memory_rw.c delivers a failed-device exception for MIPS and M88K only, and this is ARM.\n\nNOT MEASURED and no witness built; the rung-3 attempt is owed.  Note a witness needs a coprocessor read from an ARM guest, which is a different vehicle from the device probes this project mostly writes -- gate_arm.sh's 358 decode checks are the nearest existing machinery.\n\n*** DESIGN REVIEW 2026-09-10: ACCEPT IS RATIFIED, BUT MY SIBLING WARRANT WAS OVERCLAIMED AND IS NARROWED HERE.  Verified locally before recording. ***\n\nI wrote that the adjacent `case 4:' (isteer) 'carries NO fatal() and NO exit()'.  **FALSE ABOUT THE WHOLE CASE.**  Its READ arm is quiet, as I said -- but its STORE arm assigns the field and then `exit(1)' on ANY NONZERO VALUE.\n\n**THE ASYMMETRY I RELIED ON SURVIVES, AND ONLY IN THE READ DIRECTION:** case 4 READS quietly while case 0 READS and dies, which is the outlier evidence.  In the WRITE direction the two swap over -- case 0 writes quietly, case 4 writes and dies on nonzero.  So the file has kills in both cases, on opposite halves, and the correct statement is that THE READ HALVES ARE ASYMMETRIC, not that one case is clean and the other is not.\n\nThe verdict stands with a narrower warrant: **ACCEPT for the ordinary interrupt-enable read into a general register.**  Its sibling's write-side kill is a SEPARATE defect on a separate arm and belongs to a separate row, not folded into this one.\n\n*** THE OTHER SEVEN EXITS IN THIS FILE ARE TRIAGED, 2026-09-10 (xscaleinten/codex_coproc_20260910.txt, ZERO cyber flags) -- AND MY WORKING HYPOTHESIS IS REFUTED. ***\n\nI asked which of the other seven share this row's shape, on the theory that 'the value is already computed and then discarded' would make several of them one cheap round.  **NONE OF THE OTHER SEVEN HAS THE SAME WARRANTED TWO-LINE ACCEPT REPAIR.**  That is a useful negative: it stops a round copying this disposition across the file, which is exactly what the option-set rule warns about when a precedent is transferred without checking each site.\n\n**AND THE CENSUS UNDERCOUNTED THE FILE: eight ACTIVE exits plus SIX COMMENTED-OUT ones.**  The commented six are inert and are not defects, but they are worth knowing about -- a round de-fatalising a live site sits beside six places where someone already made that call and left the corpse in.\n\nTHE STRUCTURE, which is what decides how the remaining seven split into rounds: three CP15 sites, four CP6, one CP14 -- or by SHAPE, five specific-case sites, TWO DEFAULT-FED ENDPOINTS and one ENTRY GUARD.  The two default-fed ones are the priorities: CP6's shared `unknown:' label is reached from THREE defaults (the outer crm switch and both nested crn switches) and CP14's from FOUR plus a separate opcode2 guard -- so one repair at each label covers many decode paths at once.\n\n*** ONE SITE IS NEITHER A CASE NOR A DEFAULT, which no other exit in this queue has been: CP15's `rd == ARM_PC' ENTRY GUARD, before the switch, in BOTH directions. ***  A refusal that fires before any dispatch is a different fix shape from every other member of this class, and it is ranked last for that reason rather than because it is unimportant.\n\n*** REACHABILITY ANSWERED **YES** 2026-09-10 (xscaleinten/codex_witness_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, ZERO cyber flags), AND MY NEGATIVE PREMISE WAS FALSE.  This row now has a ratified disposition, a confirmed path, and a named vehicle -- it needs a measuring seat and nothing else. ***\n\n**INSTALLERS: exactly two, and both assign BEFORE the `!machine->prom_emulation' early return**, so disabling PROM emulation does not remove CP6 -- `machine_iq80321.c' (default CPU `80321_600_2') and `machine_iyonix.c' (`80321_600_B0'), each a direct `cpu->cd.arm.coproc[6] = ...'.  No other installer or caller exists.  **Selecting an XScale CPU is NOT enough**: generic ARM init installs CP15 and, for XScale, CP14 -- never CP6.  Note the setup receives the BOOTSTRAP cpu only.\n\n**PATH:** guest fetch -> `arm_instr_to_be_translated' -> condition-selected `arm_instr_mcr_mrc' -> `arm_mcr_mrc' -> `coproc[6]' -> this handler.  **No privilege or mode check anywhere in that chain** -- dispatch does not consult CPAR (stored and read through CP15, and the source says it is otherwise unused).  Cold init already selects SVC32 with IRQ/FIQ masked, so no privilege transition is needed.  Prerequisites are ordinary: the condition must pass, the fetch must succeed, ARM state not Thumb, and CP6 must be installed -- with a null entry `arm_mcr_mrc' prints a generic TODO and **its undefined-exception call and `exit(1)' are COMMENTED OUT**, so absence does not terminate.\n\n*** MY NEGATIVE PREMISE -- 'the machinery is store/load-oriented and nothing drives a coprocessor transfer' -- IS MEASURABLY FALSE. ***  FIVE existing probes already execute CP15 MRC/MCR on `cats': `footbridge_sites_probe.py', `fbpending_witness.py', `fbpending_drain_probe.py', `pit8253_latch_probe.py', and gate 14's `arm_flags_probe.py' on `testarm'.  **The directly reusable pattern is `footbridge_sites_probe.py'** -- launch a stock machine into the debugger, plant instructions in RAM, set pc, step, check disassembly, inspect registers -- pointed at IQ80321 or Iyonix instead of `cats'.  **No machine or device addition is required**, which is what the witness ladder demands.  No dedicated CP6 test exists yet.\n\n*** AN ORACLE TRAP THAT APPLIES FAR BEYOND THIS ROW: A PRINTED STEP DISASSEMBLY DOES NOT PROVE THE INSTRUCTION EXECUTED, OR EVEN THAT ITS CONDITION PASSED.  The engine prints the disassembly BEFORE executing. ***  Any probe here that treats a stepped disassembly line as evidence of execution is asserting something the engine does not promise.\n\n**A NAIVE `write W, read, expect W' HAS FOUR INDISTINGUISHABLE FAILURES:** always-zero (passes when `W' is zero); a stale value equal to `W'; a read that never executes (passes when the destination already holds `W' -- *especially easy if the write-source register is reused as the read destination*); and a program that never ran at all, when the inspected register or memory already holds the expected value.  **The discriminating form:** two DISTINCT NONZERO values written and read in succession, the read destination deliberately pre-set to a third value before each read, and SEPARATE write-source and read-destination registers.  Plus execution evidence -- intended address, decoded coprocessor and register fields, the destination TRANSITION, and continuation -- with missing observations failing rather than defaulting to zero.\n\n**AND THE ORACLE NEEDS CONTINUATION AND SILENCE, not just a value:** the CURRENT code already assigns the destination before terminating, so the assignment alone is not the outcome under test.  Unchanged source predicts the named diagnostic and exit status 1; the corrected behaviour requires the right value, continued execution, and NO diagnostic from this read arm.\n\n**THE SAME VEHICLE REACHES EVERY OTHER TERMINATION IN THIS FUNCTION** -- interrupt steering (`crn=4', write, nonzero), interrupt acknowledge (`crn=8', write, ANY value including zero), and the shared `unknown:' label (either direction, unsupported `crn' or `crm').  `opcode1' and `opcode2' are NOT validated, so changing them alone does not route a recognised selection to `unknown'.  *** EACH SITE NEEDS ITS OWN PROCESS INVOCATION, because the first `exit(1)' reached prevents observing any later one. ***  That groups the COVERAGE work without implying the repairs should be identical.\n\n**A COUNT IN MY OWN BRIEF WAS WRONG: `crm=1' has SIXTEEN implemented direction arms -- eight reads and eight writes -- not fourteen.**  All sixteen are locally quiet, but *** 'quiet' DOES NOT MEAN 'a plain storage assignment' ***: `tcr0' and `tcr1' INCREMENT before returning their values, and a `tisr' write clears selected bits and deasserts timer interrupts.  A sibling-parity argument resting on 'the timer arms are plain accessors' would be wrong.\n\n*** 2026-09-11: WITNESS DESIGNED (rung 3, `-E iq80321`, six invocations).  Two of my stated premises were WRONG and the seat corrected both; one architectural fact it established changes what the round may CLAIM. ***\n\n**THE DEBUGGER CANNOT REACH CP6 STATE AT ALL, so clause (f)'s anchor rule cannot be satisfied in its usual form here.**  `debugger_cmd_reg()` rejects any coprocessor number outside 0-3 -- coproc 6 cannot even be asked for; `arm_cpu_register_dump()` prints GPRs, cpsr, banked registers and the CP15 control block and **names no CP6 field anywhere**; and the settings tree carries only `pc`, `r0`-`r9`/`sl`/`fp`/`ip`/`sp`/`lr` and `cpsr`, so `print` cannot reach it either.\n\n> **The ONLY way to observe `cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten` is to make a guest MRC copy it into a GPR -- which is the instruction under test.  There is no channel OUTSIDE the decode under test.**  The three substitutes (the MCR store arm at `:315` as a different branch naming the same lvalue; the `unknown:` printf anchoring the ENCODING; the crm 1 `trr0` bank anchoring BANK SELECTION) are each real and **none is decode-independent.  The contract must say so rather than claim an anchor it does not have.**\n\nMY WRONG PREMISES, both corrected by reading: (1) I said five existing probes execute CP15 MRC/MCR **on `cats`** -- `arm_flags_probe.py` is `-E testarm`, and its CP15 word `0xEE070F9A` is an MCR that ARMS A COMBINER, not a register read.  The substance held; the machine attribution did not.  (2) I proposed `cats` as reachable -- it is not: generic ARM init installs only CP15/CP14, so `arm_mcr_mrc` takes its `else` branch, **prints and does NOT exit**, leaving the poison unchanged and the step line disassembling a perfectly good `mrc 6,...`.  *That state is pixel-identical to 'the instruction did not execute', which is why row A2 asserts the ABSENCE of `[ TODO: arm_mcr_mrc:` with `cp_num=6`.  Without it the two conflate.*\n\nTHE BLIND SPOT I FLAGGED IS ANSWERED BY A ROW, NOT A COMMENT.  **P2** asserts, from the step-PRE snapshot, that each subject destination still holds its POISON immediately before its own step; if any already holds the expected value the probe prints `VOID` for every value row and goes RED.  **X4** is its inequality companion: arm N (subject word replaced by a NOP) must NOT show `S1` in r3 -- if it does, something other than the instruction under test put it there and every value row is worthless.\n\nTHE DISASSEMBLY GAP IS CLOSED BY MEASUREMENT, NOT BY ARGUMENT.  `cpu_dyntrans.c:282-296` re-reads and disassembles the word, and executes it afterwards at `:325`; the print path never consults the condition code.  So **arm C** plants `0x0E103610` -- ONE NIBBLE from the subject word -- and the step line prints `mrceq 6,0,r3,cr0,cr0,0` while r3 must NOT change.  *One word, one nibble apart, opposite answers: that is the statement that a printed CP6 MRC and an executed one are different events.*\n\nENCODING, derived twice rather than recalled: `MRC p6,0,r3,c0,c0,0` = **0xEE103610**, from ARM ARM DDI 0406C.d A8.8.108 (`ddi0406.txt:25478`) AND from the emulator's own field extractor at `cpu_arm.c:2645-2651`.  The two agree field for field.  General form `0xEE100610 | (crn<<16) | (rd<<12) | crm`.\n\nSIX INVOCATIONS, and the rule is stated rather than assumed: **one per terminating site, plus one per mutually-exclusive content of the subject slot.**  Arms E (`unknown:` at `:419`) and S (isrc write at `:333`) terminate in BOTH builds, so neither can share a process with anything downstream; D and L terminate pre-fix only; N and C are the same slot with different words.  **S is the blast-radius control: if it ever goes green the deletion was not scoped to one arm, and that is a FAIL.**\n\nFOUR OF SIX ARMS MUST BE BYTE-IDENTICAL ACROSS THE TWO BUILDS -- itself a measured statement of the diff's blast radius.\n\n(d) NULL-REPAIR: the fix adds no predicate, so the mutants reroute existing ones.  `if (l_bit)` -> `if (l_bit && cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten)` is caught by **V0 alone**, the reset read taken while the field is still 0 -- which is why V0 is in the program and was not dropped for brevity.  A kill re-added on a narrow `rd` is caught by the five distinct subject `rd` values {3,4,5,6,9}, `rd` being a floor axis because it indexes the guarded body's own lvalue.\n\nDISCLOSED (clause (e)): predicates on `opcode1`/`opcode2` -- every word here has both at 0, and the body never TESTS them (it only passes them to the `unknown:` printf), so they are not floor axes here.  Also disclosed: interrupt DELIVERY is unreachable from a stepping probe (`debugger.c:800`, `:835-840` stop the host timer), so this is necessarily a REGISTER-TRANSFER detector, not a behaviour detector.  And the `isteer`-nonzero kill at `:324` is the one remaining `crm 0` kill this detector does not observe.\n\nDISPOSITION UNCHANGED AND STRENGTHENED.  `:311` already computes the correct value before killing, and `dev_i80321.c:445-446` aliases the field into the interrupt controller (`d->enable`), read at `:86`/`:92` -- so it is live state a consumer reads, and an *\"unimplemented; ignored\"* message would be false in product code.  The sibling grep agrees: within `crm 0` the `crn 4` READ at `:318` is already a silent accept and the `crm 1` bank is silent both ways, so silencing `crn 0` makes the file MORE internally consistent.\n\nNO MMU PROLOGUE AND NO ARM T, deliberately: a coprocessor move touches no memory, so the only access is the instruction fetch at VA 0x8000, which `arm_setup_initial_translation_table(cpu, 0x4000)` maps identically.  **The probe header must say this in those words** -- a reader who knows `footbridge_sites_probe.py` will look for its arm T and must not conclude it was forgotten.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 ADVERSARIAL PASS 1.  **THE ANCHOR ADMISSION IS HALF FALSE, AND FINDING THE MISSED CHANNEL CONVERTS A DISCLOSED WEAKNESS INTO A CLOSED ONE.**  One FLOOR BREACH (L2) and one TOTAL ESCAPE were found; all seven encodings verified correct. ***\n\n**THE DEBUGGER HALF IS CONFIRMED, line by line:** `debugger_cmds.c:1662` rejects any coprocessor number outside 0-3 (`reg ,6` is refused); `cpu_arm.c:193-198` registers only `pc`, the GPRs and `cpsr`; `arm_cpu_register_dump` names no CP6 field; and `i80321_inten` appears in exactly three places in `src/` -- `cpu_arm_coproc.c:311,315`, `dev_i80321.c:446`, `cpu_arm.h:212`.  `DEVICE_ACCESS(i80321)` never touches `d->enable`, so there is **no MMIO alias**.\n\n*** BUT \"NO CHANNEL OUTSIDE THE DECODE UNDER TEST\" IS FALSE. ***\n\n    dev_i80321.c:446    d->enable = &cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten;\n    dev_i80321.c:84-87  { *d->status |= linemask;\n                          if (*d->status & *d->enable) INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq); }\n\n**`i80321_assert()` is a SECOND READER of the exact cell, outside the CP6 decode, and its observable is the ARM IRQ line.  That is clause (f)'s \"side effect\" anchor, verbatim** -- and it is reachable SYNCHRONOUSLY inside one guest store: `machine_iq80321.c:61-64` wires the ns16550 as `irq=...i80321.28`, and `DEVICE_ACCESS(ns16550)` calls `dev_ns16550_tick()` inside the guest's own store for `com_ier` and `com_mcr`.  So `strb 0x02 -> 0xfe800001` then `strb 0x08 -> 0xfe800004` asserts line 28 and the `isrc & inten` test fires.\n\n**THE COST, stated honestly: the anchor arm must FREE-RUN.**  `cpu_dyntrans.c:205-209` suppresses the exception under `single_step`, and `irq_asserted` is in neither the `reg` dump nor the settings tree, so a stepping arm cannot see it.  Use the project's existing free-running shape: clear CPSR I, plant `b .`, breakpoint at **0x18** (`ARM_EXCEPTION_IRQ = 6`, and `ARM_CONTROL_V` is not in the reset set), `continue`.  Contrast row: `inten = 0` gives no exception and the `b .` spins to the stall budget.\n\n=== M1 -- A FLOOR BREACH (L2), AND IT IS THE ANSWER TO THE QUESTION I MOST WANTED ===\n\n    if (l_bit) { r[rd] = inten;\n                 if (cpu->cd.arm.i80321_isrc != 0) { fatal(...); exit(1); } }\n\n**`i80321_isrc` is 0 in every one of the six arms** -- nothing asserts an i80321 interrupt in a bare probe, and the only guest-reachable writer of `isrc` is the crn-8 MCR, which `exit(1)`s.  **On a real guest it is NEVER 0 at the moment INTCTL is read, because NetBSD reads INTCTL from inside the interrupt path.**  The host dies exactly as before and nothing in the six arms can see it.\n\nGraded L2 rather than clause-(e) debt under the pass-11 generating-set ruling: `inten`'s ONLY consumer is `i80321_assert`/`i80321_deassert`, which reads `*d->status & *d->enable`, so `isrc` and `inten` are a JOINT INPUT of the model, written by different paths.  The floor is `{l_bit} x {inten classes} x {isrc = 0, != 0}` and **the whole `isrc != 0` column is empty.**  *The same anchor arm closes it: asserting the serial gives `isrc = 1<<28`, readable back through `MRC p6,0,Rt,c8,c0,0` = `0xEE187610` -- the crn-8 LOAD has no `exit(1)` (`:328-329`), a fact the design did not use.*\n\n=== M5 -- A TOTAL ESCAPE, AND IT IS NOT A HOST KILL, WHICH MAKES IT WORSE ===\n\nSwap the cell on BOTH sides to `i80321_isteer`: reset read 0, three round-trips, L, N, C, E and S all GREEN -- **while the device's interrupt enable mask is never written and a guest's interrupts silently never fire.**  The design's own anchor admission conceded this was invisible; with the channel above found, it is closable.\n\n=== THREE MORE ESCAPES, EACH CLOSED BY ONE WORD ===\n* **M2 -- the detector's real bound is FOUR READS PER PROCESS.**  Arm D performs exactly four subject reads and L performs one, each in a fresh process, so a `static int nread; if (++nread > 4) exit(1);` escapes 6/6 while a guest polling INTCTL dies.  Repair: one more read in arm D.\n* **M3 -- the `rd` axis is not closed, and the manual says WHICH value matters.**  DDI 0406 A8.8.108: *\"`<Rt>` ... can be R0-R14 or, in some cases, APSR_nzcv\"* -- **Rt = R15 means write the CONDITION FLAGS**, and GXemul writes `r[15]`, the PC, unconditionally.  That is a real modelling defect, so a future `if (rd == ARM_PC) exit(1)` is likely.  `{3,4,5,6,9}` is five of sixteen and **omits the one value with an architectural special case**.  `0xEE10F610`.\n* **M4 -- `opcode1`/`opcode2` are ignored by the arm and CONSTANT across all six invocations.**  `if (opcode2 != 0) exit(1)` escapes 6/6 and re-opens the kill for 7/8 of the encoding space.  Close with `0xEE10A630`.\n\n=== CORRECTIONS TO THE DESIGN ===\n* **A FOURTH TERMINATING SITE IS UNROWED**: crn 4 STORE with a non-zero value (`:320-325`).  It is the subject's nearest sibling inside the same `case crm 0:` switch, and under SIBLING PARITY crn 4 is uncovered in BOTH directions -- including the silent-accept read at `:317-318` that the disposition argument leans on.\n* **L, N and C MERGE into one invocation** -- the rule binds only if the words must occupy the same address, and nothing requires that.  Six arms -> four, before the additions.\n* **Arm E is strengthened for free**: `0xEE107612` yields three ZERO fields, so a broken extractor still mostly matches.  **`0xEE79B6B2`** gives `opcode1=3 opcode2=5 crn=9 crm=2 rd=11 l=1` -- six distinct non-trivial fields, turning E into a decode-field anchor at zero cost.\n* **THE NOP WAS NAMED, NOT ENCODED.**  *\"the NOP (`mov r0,r0`)\"* is a LABEL; the word is `0xE1A00000`.  That is precisely this project's hand-assembled-encoding trap, recorded four times.\n* **MY NON-INTERFERENCE REASON WAS WRONG AND THE CONCLUSION SURVIVES.**  I said the tick cannot fire inside a `step` because its input is a `timer_add` callback.  **The tick DOES fire** -- `machine.c:354` initialises `ticks_till_next[n] = 0` and `:727-730` fires at `<= 0`, so every tick function runs on step 1 of every invocation.  What protects the cell is the ELSE branch: `tmr0` is 0 at reset, so the else writes `i80321_isrc` and `tisr` and never `inten`.  *This matters for the free-running anchor arm, which must not assume the tick is inert -- that same step-1 tick calls `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT` whenever `!(isrc & inten)`.*\n\n=== VERIFIED SOUND ===  All seven encodings confirmed against BOTH DDI 0406 A8.8.108/A8.8.99 and the emulator's own extractor, field for field, with three near-miss combiner traps cleared (`mia*`, `0xee070f9a`, `0x8afffffa`).  VA 0x8000 verified and **more robust than claimed** -- it holds even under `-Q`, because with the MMU off VA == PA.  *But the margin is ZERO: the L1 table ends at 0x7FFF and the code starts at 0x8000, so any literal pool below the code lands in the table.*  Disposition confirmed as option-set member **4 ACCEPT**, the DC21285 `+0x188` precedent exactly.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- WITNESS BUILT AND MEASURED (agy 3.8, scratch clone).  **THE ANCHOR ARM IS THE ONLY THING THAT KILLS M1 AND M5, MEASURED.** ***\n\n    witness, committed HEAD   SCORE 11/26   (15 rows red)\n    witness, with the fix     SCORE 26/26\n    BASE 26/26   RESTORED 26/26   restore verified BOTH parts (cmp CLEAN, binary FRESH)\n\n**THE KILL TABLE VINDICATES THE ADVERSARIAL PASS BY MEASUREMENT, which is the whole point of having built the anchor arm:**\n\n    M1        23/26   FAILING: ANCHOR_M1, ANCHOR_EXC, ANCHOR_CONTRAST\n    M5        25/26   FAILING: ANCHOR_EXC\n    M_OPCODE2 19/26   M_RD15 25/26   M_NREAD 19/26\n\n> **`M1` -- the floor breach -- dies on NOTHING BUT THE THREE ANCHOR ROWS.  `M5` -- the total escape that is not a host kill -- dies on EXACTLY ONE ROW, `ANCHOR_EXC`.**  Every stepping arm is green under both.  *So without the free-running anchor arm the six-arm design would have shipped with a floor breach and a total escape, exactly as the review predicted from reading alone.*\n\nThat is this project's *a reading seat buys the CLASS, a measuring seat buys the INSTANCE* rule with both halves present in one day: the review named `M1` and `M5` from the source and said the stepping arms could not see them; the table shows they are seen by three rows and one row respectively, and by nothing else.\n\n`M_OPCODE2` and `M_NREAD` each red the same seven D-arm rows (19/26), and `M_RD15` reds exactly `RD15_SURVIVE` (25/26) -- so the three cheap one-word additions the review prescribed each carry a measured kill rather than being precautionary.\n\n**ORDER: the PRETABLE discriminator HOLDS** -- `PRETABLE.out` at `02:18:05`, `build_BASE.log` at `02:20:51`, so the block was written 2m46s before table 1 and its own mtime proves it.\n\n*** BUT ONE ORDER CLAIM IS NOT EVIDENCED AND I WILL NOT ASSERT IT: whether the COMMITTED witness leg was run BEFORE the edit was applied. ***  `witness_fix.out` carries mtime `02:20:10` and `witness_committed.out` `02:20:23` -- **the fix leg is the EARLIER file** -- and the report does not address ordering anywhere (grep for order/test-first/before-any-edit returns nothing).\n\n**What IS established is weaker but real: the kill table's own `HEAD` leg checks out the committed source, rebuilds and probes, scoring 11/26.**  So a pre-fix measurement exists and is reproducible; what is missing is evidence that it was taken FIRST, which is what witness-ladder clause (i) asks for.  *This project's oracle-correction rule exists because a measurement taken after the edit cannot be distinguished from one fitted to it -- and the two mtimes here are the only thing that speaks to the question, and they point the wrong way.*\n\n**OWED: either a statement from the run that the committed leg preceded the edit, or a re-run of the committed leg from a clean checkout with its mtime recorded.**  Cheap either way, and it is the difference between a witness and a detector in this project's own vocabulary.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PROPAGATION CHECK (two-part clearance, RUN not asserted).  **ITS SEC EDIT IS IN NO BUILD TREE, AND ALL THREE BINARIES REPORT `FRESH`.** ***\n\n`cpu_arm_coproc.c` carries this row's uncommitted SEC edit (2 lines removed).  The clearance was run against all three build trees and `est/`:\n\n    tree               part 1 (cmp vs SEC)   part 2 [binary -nt source]\n    build/             DIVERGENT             FRESH   (binary 00:48:13)\n    build-asan/        DIVERGENT             FRESH   (binary 00:51:01)\n    /tmp/gxsec-build   DIVERGENT             FRESH   (binary 00:51:42)\n    est/               SOURCE tree -- no binary exists, part 2 N/A (inapplicable, not unrun)\n\n*The other three edited files in the same working tree -- `cpu_ppc_instr.c`, `dev_lca.c`, `dev_sgi_re.c` -- are CLEAN in all three, so this is a single missed propagation rather than a tree-wide drift.*\n\n> *** THE DANGEROUS PART IS THAT PART 2 GIVES NO WARNING: all three binaries are NEWER than their sources, so `FRESH` is TRUE and MEANINGLESS -- the binary is newer than a source that is simply the wrong source. ***  **Part 2 tests staleness against whatever source sits in the tree; only part 1 tests whether that is the source you think it is.**  A round that ran only the freshness half here would measure PRE-FIX ARM coprocessor behaviour and see nothing wrong.\n\n**None of the four files is in the divergence set** (derived, 6 paths), so the clearance genuinely applies and `DIVERGENT` here means UNPROPAGATED rather than by-design.\n\n*** NOT PROPAGATED IN THIS PASS, DELIBERATELY. ***  Propagating without rebuilding is the trap this project has paid for twice, and a build was in flight (agy, `wdcaltdigin` witness).  **Propagate and rebuild together, then re-run BOTH parts** -- recorded in `_scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md` with the restore recipe so a fresh session finds it.\n\n*Found by running the clearance at a round boundary rather than at a mutation boundary -- which is the carrier's own finding that the two-part clearance is a ROUND-BOUNDARY obligation and that the sentinel discipline cannot catch this class at all, because nothing mutated anything.*",
44291:   "witness": "probe:regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/agy_xscale_witness_20260911.py -- rung 3, cold-debugger, UNMODIFIED in-tree machine.  RED ON THE COMMITTED BUILD AND GREEN ON THE FIX, SAME SCRIPT -- but test-first ORDER is NOT established (witness_fix.out carries the EARLIER mtime, 02:20:10 v 02:20:23; corrected 2026-09-12 from this field's earlier 'TEST-FIRST SATISFIED'); every kill table's HEAD leg reproduces the death from a clean rebuild (11/26, 12/29, 12/30): witness_committed.out SCORE 11/26 (FAILING D_TODO_ABS, D_SURVIVE, D_RESET, D_S1, D_ZERO, D_S2, D_OP2, D_MARKER, LNC_TODO_ABS, LNC_SUB, LNC_MARKER, RD15_SURVIVE, ANCHOR_M1, ANCHOR_EXC, ANCHOR_CONTRAST) and witness_fix.out SCORE 26/26 FAILING NONE.",
