44464:   "note": "FILED 2026-09-10 from the GPT-6 Astra exit audit (regress/artifacts/queuetriage/codex_exitaudit_20260910.txt), which listed it among the six E1 predicates.  VERIFIED LOCALLY by opening the file.\n\nThe handler is a bare `if (len == 8) {...} else if (len == 4) {...} else { fatal(...); exit(1); }'.  There is no other width arm, so EVERY other access length -- 1, 2, and anything wider -- reaches the kill.  A byte load is an ordinary thing for a guest or a PROM to do while probing, and nothing about the device's mapping advertises a width restriction.\n\nREACHABILITY: machine_sgi.c installs it with `dev_crime_init(machine, mem, 0x14000000, ...)' on the IP32 machines.  CRIME is the memory and interrupt controller, i.e. one of the first things any IP32 guest touches.\n\n*** THE DISPOSITION IS THE INTERESTING PART, because member 5 (FAULT) IS GENUINELY AVAILABLE HERE, which it usually is not. ***  memory_rw.c converts a failed device callback into an architectural access exception for MIPS and M88K only -- and this device is on MIPS.  So `return 0' is not a silent decline here: it is a positive statement that the access could not be serviced, delivered to the guest as the bus error real hardware would give.  That is the dev_unreadable.c idiom the option set records, and machine_sgi.c already installs dev_unreadable ten times for exactly this reason -- the PROM and NetBSD probe absent regions and RELY on the failure.  A round here should weigh FAULT against DECLINE rather than defaulting to decline as most of this queue does.\n\nNOT MEASURED, no witness.  The rung-3 attempt is owed and looks cheap: an IP32 machine with a single byte load at 0x14000000 through the debugger.\n\n*** DESIGN REVIEW 2026-09-10 (faultpair/codex_faultpair_20260910.txt): THE VERDICT GOES AGAINST MY LEANING -- DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, **NOT** FAULT -- and the distinction is the useful part. ***\n\nI filed this row arguing that FAULT is available here and should be weighed first.  The review agrees it is MECHANICALLY available and rules against using it anyway: *'the evidence does not justify treating either entire refusal predicate as a hardware bus-error condition.'*  **AVAILABLE IS NOT THE SAME AS RIGHT.**  dev_unreadable.c's precedent is about a positively ABSENT device, and this is a PRESENT device refusing one access width -- returning a machine check for a width the model simply has not implemented asserts something about the hardware that nothing here establishes.\n\n*** AND MY REACHABILITY STORY WAS TOO NARROW, which sharpens the row rather than weakening it. ***  I wrote 'any len that is not 4 or 8'.  The review establishes the population properly: ordinary MIPS scalar accesses deliver 1, 2, 4 or 8 bytes, and the load/store handler checks ALIGNMENT before calling memory_rw -- **which does not exclude byte accesses or aligned halfwords**, so 1 and 2 are ordinary guest traffic.  The unaligned left/right instructions use aligned 4- or 8-byte transactions and do NOT manufacture odd callback lengths.  **But EIGHT BYTES IS NOT A GLOBAL CALLBACK MAXIMUM:** IP32 also installs MEC, whose transmit-descriptor reader passes a THIRTY-TWO-BYTE buffer through physical memory_rw, and that path is not restricted to RAM.  So wider CRIME callbacks cannot be recorded as dead on the strength of scalar instruction widths, and `len' is a size_t rather than an unconstrained guest integer but is not universally {1,2,4,8} either.\n\nSo the round is a DECLINE with a latched announce, and its detector must cover BOTH the small widths (ordinary scalar traffic) and at least one wide callback (the descriptor path).\n\n*** THE PREMISE I CALLED MY LEAST CERTAIN IS SETTLED, BY READING TWO LINES -- AND THE READING SEAT REFUSED THE QUESTION TWICE.  2026-09-10. ***\n\n**`memory_rw.c` PASSES THE GUEST'S ACCESS WIDTH TO THE DEVICE CALLBACK UNCHANGED:**\n\n        res = mem->devices[i].f(cpu, mem, paddr,\n            data, len, writeflag,\n            mem->devices[i].extra);\n\nThere is no widening, splitting or alignment at that call site, so a guest one-byte load arrives at `DEVICE_ACCESS(crime)' with `len == 1', falls past both width arms and reaches the terminating call.  **The route is confirmed by construction of the dispatcher, not by a measurement** -- what is still owed is the rung-3 witness that actually drives it.\n\n*** TWO CODEX REFUSALS ON THIS ONE SUBJECT, AND THE SECOND KILLED MY OWN EXPLANATION OF THE FIRST. ***  Run 1 was refused with the whole-`regress` scope and a question asking for a census of terminating calls across the file; I hypothesised the census ask was the trigger.  Run 2 dropped the census question AND narrowed the scope to five named files -- **refused again, in the same place, with ZERO `regress/artifacts` hits.**\n\nThe log says what it was doing: reading **`memory_rw.c`** when the flag fired, 45 termination tokens in its own reasoning, none from an archive.  **The reading that survives the pair is that asking a seat to TRACE A GUEST-CHOSEN INPUT THROUGH THE MEMORY DISPATCHER TO A HOST-TERMINATION SITE is the trigger** -- with [[xscaleinten]] as the near-control, since it asked the same can-an-ordinary-instruction-reach-this question, in the same session, with the same scoping paragraph, and came back clean because its path runs through a handler TABLE rather than through memory dispatch.\n\n*** SO THIS QUESTION IS NOT CODEX'S, AND IT COST TWO INVOCATIONS TO LEARN. ***  A width-reachability trace is something a MEASURING seat settles by measuring -- drive the byte load on an unmodified IP32 machine and see -- faster than a reading seat takes to refuse it.  **DO NOT RE-FIRE A THIRD TIME**: two refusals on two differently-worded briefs is a seat declining the SUBJECT, and the re-fire-once rule is about WALLS, not refusals.  Both refusal logs are preserved.\n\nWHAT THE ROW NEEDS NOW, in order: a rung-3 witness on an unmodified IP32 machine (one guest byte load at `0x14000000` through the debugger), then the ratified repair -- decline the unsupported width with a bounded latched announce -- then a detector whose oracle separates a correct decline from (a) a handler answering zero at every width, (b) one that quietly services the byte against the wrong register, and (c) a run where the access never happened.  **A bare 'the emulator kept running' assertion sees none of those three**, which is the survive-only vacuity this project has measured before.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 RECORDS NOTE: **THE FILE IS `dev_sgi_ip32.c`**, and this row has always said so -- but two readers in one day reached for a `dev_crime.c` that does not exist. ***\n\nThere is **no `dev_crime.c` anywhere under the project root**, in the fork or in any of the three 2021-vintage upstream copies; the only CRIME file is `src/include/thirdparty/crimereg.h`.  The width kill is `src/devices/dev_sgi_ip32.c`, `DEVICE_ACCESS(crime)`, with `fatal(\"crime access len = %i!\")` at `:291` inside the `else` of an `if (len == 8) / else if (len == 4)` chain at `:269`/`:274`/`:290-292`, and `machine_sgi.c:497` calls `dev_crime_init(...0x14000000...)`.  **Verified by opening the file.**\n\n**The trap is the NAME**: the row id is `crimewidthkill`, the constructor is `dev_crime_init()`, and the header is `crimereg.h` -- three pointers to a filename that does not exist.  A brief of mine listed `dev_crime.c` as a permitted file, and an auditing agent's writeup cited `dev_crime.c:269` while its LINE NUMBERS were exactly right for `dev_sgi_ip32.c`, i.e. it read the correct file and mislabelled it.\n\n*Worth keeping because the failure is cheap to repeat and invisible when it succeeds: the agent's content was sound, so nothing in its report looked wrong.  **A citation whose FILE is wrong and whose LINES are right reads as more authoritative than one that is merely vague.***",
47124:   "note": "FILED 2026-09-10.  Evidence: regress/artifacts/queuetriage/codex_exitaudit_20260910.txt (GPT-6 Astra at max), which enumerated the population properly for the first time.  **This row exists so the next session reads the census instead of re-running it**, which has now happened twice.\n\n*** THE EARLIER FIGURES WERE RIGHT AND THEIR DESCRIPTION WAS WRONG: 370 and 240 count `exit(1)', NOT every `exit()'. ***  Re-derived by removing comments and string literals while preserving line positions, then locating the calls, with an independent character-state lexer returning exactly the same positions:\n\n    src/devices    263 literal `exit('   135 calls after lexical filtering\n    src/cpus       112                    74\n    src/machines    35                    34\n    TOTAL          410                   243   (240 of them exit(1))\n\nLOCATION SPLIT of those 243: 150 runtime handlers and helpers, 80 init/configuration, 13 CPU generators and a standalone experiment.  The debugger is outside that scope and adds three more.\n\nAND BEING INSIDE A RUNTIME FUNCTION IS NOT REACHABILITY.  The 150 split: **98 input-reachable through enabled in-tree wiring**; 33 dead, precluded, or not established reachable; 10 optional instrumentation; 6 unattached or disabled attachments; 2 intentional halt/reboot; 1 host RAM-config check misplaced in an access handler.\n\nOWNERSHIP is an OVERLAY, not a disjoint population -- 22 live exits and 3 others are actively owned (n6 holds eight footbridge sites; alphavmkill one; sgirekills five; lcaisalane and lcabadval eight between them; m88kexit two dead ones).  **A strict open/held filter leaves 83, of which seven belong to in-work LCA repairs, so THE UNOWNED REMAINDER IS 76.**\n\n*** AND ONE OF MY OWN EARLIER QUALIFICATIONS WAS TOO AGGRESSIVE, corrected here: lacking a separate CHILD repair id does not mean lacking an open OWNING row.  `sgirekills' explicitly retains its five command exits.  Conversely an incidental mention in census prose is NOT a repair row. ***\n\nSIX E1 PREDICATES were identified -- a one-to-five-line change with an existing test carrier -- and ALL SIX ARE NOW FILED as their own rows: [[mk48nvramkill]], [[mapledmaaddr]], [[xscaleinten]], [[crimewidthkill]], [[vmedefaultkill]], [[fbctrlportkill]].  **This row therefore tracks the ~70 that remain, and they are E2/E3: real rounds, not quick wins.**  The heaviest concentrations are dev_osiop.c (14), cpu_arm_coproc.c (8 of which one is filed), dev_adb.c (7), dev_dreamcast_g2.c (6), dev_pvr.c and dev_igsfb.c (5 each).\n\nTHE ONE CONSTRAINT THAT GOVERNS EVERY DISPOSITION IN THIS BODY, and it is easy to forget: **memory_rw.c turns a failed device callback into an architectural access exception for MIPS and M88K ONLY.**  Returning zero from an ARM or SH device callback gives NO bus-error delivery, so the FAULT member is unavailable there and a round reaching for it owes the exception path first.  CPU translation and instruction handlers have their own direct mechanisms, which is a separate question.\n\n*** THE LARGEST CONCENTRATION HAS BEEN TRIAGED, 2026-09-10: dev_osiop.c (osiopexits/codex_triage_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max; 3 cyber flags, ALL MID-STREAM and non-fatal). ***\n\n**THE FILE HAS SIXTEEN EXPLICIT exit() CALLS, NOT FOURTEEN.**  The census's fourteen are all still executable, and two more exist that it did not list -- **and those two are the ones that turn out to be DEAD**, so the census happened to enumerate exactly the live set without saying why.\n\n    (i)   guest register access or guest-started SCRIPTS      **14**  -- one register default,\n                                                                        thirteen sequencer exits\n    (ii)  unconstructible state or impossible branch          **2**\n    (iii) internal failures where stopping is right           **0**\n\n*** POPULATION (iii) BEING EMPTY IS ITSELF THE FINDING, and it is the one that licenses the whole body of work.  NONE of the sixteen checks establishes corrupted host ownership or any other condition that requires terminating.  The seat says the diagnostic wording -- notably 'cannot be combined' -- does NOT turn these into internal assertions, which is the argument a round would otherwise have to make sixteen times. ***\n\nTHE TWO DEAD PATHS, RECORDED WITH THEIR WARRANTS so nobody re-finds them:\n  * the reserved-phase MOVE default -- an earlier equality check forces the instruction phase to match SOCL; initialisation and every implemented transition produce only handled phases; SET/CLEAR do not touch the phase bits; and a direct SOCL write terminates at the REGISTER DEFAULT before another SCRIPTS execution can run.  *That last clause is why this one is dead only while the register default is still fatal -- fixing the register default could REVIVE it, which a round must not miss.*\n  * the outer instruction-class default is **mathematically unreachable**: the switch evaluates `dcmd & 0xc0' and explicitly handles all four possible results.\n\n**AND IT CORRECTED THE AUDIT'S EFFORT RANKING:** the blanket E3 (`preserve command termination, status and interrupt behaviour') OVERSTATES the obligation for the register default and the redundant table-MOVE equality check, because the real status and completion sequence lives in the STATUS and MSG_IN phases, and the backend-failure branches are ALREADY de-fatalised by earlier rounds and sit outside this census.  So two of the nine prescribed rows are cheaper than the audit implied.\n\n*** THE SEVERITY CEILING, ASKED FOR AND ANSWERED PLAINLY: 'no in-tree runnable workload drives this controller's register/SCRIPTS interface.' ***  All fourteen are guest-reachable in principle and NOTHING IN THIS TREE EXERCISES THEM.  That is worth more than a ranking: it means these rounds cannot reach rung 4, and a witness must plant SCRIPTS by hand.\n\nA TRAP NAMED FOR THE FIRST ROW, and it is the kind that makes a one-line repair wrong: the generic byte path STORES BEFORE DISPATCH, and the word path also stores to DNAD and ADDER before reaching the unhandled-register default -- so restoring only `oldreg' would NOT undo those word stores.  And a blanket backed-accept would enable SOCL writes, **which changes the reserved-phase reachability finding above**.  The two interact.\n\nNINE REPAIR ROWS PRESCRIBED covering the fourteen, in priority order: register default; redundant table-MOVE equality check; MOVE phase mismatch; transfer-control handling; MOVE addressing modes; SELECT validation; WAIT RESELECT; register/ALU instructions; MEMORY MOVE.  Not filed individually yet -- filing nine rows for a controller nothing drives would crowd a queue that already holds live guest-reachable kills on machines this project DOES boot."
